window.EF_CRUX_DOC_PAGES=["ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.1 Introduction to Biology & the Living World\nINTRODUCTION TO BIOLOGY & THE LIVING WORLD —\nMEMORY TRICK SHEET\nGeneral Science · Biology · Chapter 1 · Sirf Yaad Rakhne Wali Cheezein — Tricks + Chains\nYe full notes nahi hai — source chapter ke coined-by vs father, branches, taxonomy, five kingdoms, Monera, symbiosis,\nvirus–viroid–prion aur biological names ko exam-recall ke liye compress kiya gaya hai.\n1. Biology Basics — Coined vs Father\n• Biology = study of living organisms\n• Greek roots: Bios = Life; Logos = Study\n• Term “Biology” coined by Lamarck & Treviranus\n• Father of Biology = Aristotle\nTRICK - “Lamarck + Treviranus TERM; Aristotle FATHER”\nCoined-by aur Father ko ek hi person mat samjho.\nBios + Logos = Life + Study.\nEXAM TRAP -\nBiology term = Lamarck & Treviranus; Father of Biology = Aristotle.\n2. Three Core Branches — B-Z-M\n• Botany\n Plants\n Theophrastus\n→\n→\n• Zoology\n Animals\n Aristotle\n→\n→\n• Microbiology\n Microbes\n Antonie van Leeuwenhoek\n→\n→\nTRICK - “B-Z-M = Botany–Zoology–Microbiology”\nFather chain: Theophrastus – Aristotle – Leeuwenhoek.\nVisual hook: Plant\n Animal\n Microbe.\n→\n→\n3. Sub-Branches — Person-to-Topic Anchors\nBranch / Source Association\nStudy\nEndocrinology — Thomas Addison\nHormones / Endocrine system\nImmunology — Edward Jenner\nImmunity\nGenetics — Gregor Mendel\nHeredity & variation\nCytology — Robert Hooke\nCells\nEugenics — Francis Galton\nHuman generations\nTaxonomy — Carolus Linnaeus\nIdentify / Name / Classify\nPaleontology — George Cuvier\nFossils\nEmbryology — Panchanan Maheshwari\nGametes, fertilisation, embryo\nAgronomy — Pietro de Crescenzi\nCrop production & soil\nAgrostology — Johann Scheuchzer\nGrasses\nAnthology\nFlowers\nSerpentology — Patrick Russell\nSnakes\nProtozoology — A. van Leeuwenhoek\nProtozoa\nTRICK - “Addison–Jenner–Mendel–Hooke–Galton–Cuvier”\nHormone = Addison | Immunity = Jenner | Genetics = Mendel | Cell = Hooke | Eugenics = Galton | Fossil = Cuvier.\nGrass = Agrostology; Snake = Serpentology; Protozoa = Protozoology.","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.1 Introduction to Biology & the Living World\n4. Taxonomy — Coined vs Father + Binomial Rules\n• Term “Taxonomy” coined by Augustin Pyramus de Candolle\n• Father of Taxonomy = Carolus Linnaeus\n• Taxonomy = Identifying + Naming + Classifying organisms\n• Binomial Nomenclature introduced by Carolus Linnaeus; source references Systema Naturae\n• Scientific name = Genus + Species\n• Genus first letter capital; species small; scientific name italicized\n• Example: Homo sapiens\n Homo = Genus; sapiens = Species\n→\nTRICK - “de Candolle COINED; Linnaeus FATHER + BINOMIAL”\nTaxonomy ka classic 3-way split: coined by de Candolle, father Linnaeus, binomial also Linnaeus.\nEXAM TRAP -\nGenus Capital + species small + whole scientific name italics.\n5. Taxonomic Hierarchy — K-P-C-O-F-G-S\n• Kingdom\n Phylum (Animals) / Division (Plants)\n Class\n Order\n Family\n Genus\n Species\n→\n→\n→\n→\n→\n→\nTRICK - “King Philip Came Over For Good Soup”\nK = Kingdom, P = Phylum, C = Class, O = Order, F = Family, G = Genus, S = Species.\nEXAM TRAP - “Herbarium = Dry + Press”\nPlants herbarium mein drying & pressing se preserve kiye jaate hain.\n6. Five Kingdom Classification — Whittaker 1969\n• Proposed by Robert Whittaker in 1969\n• Criteria = Cell Structure + Body Organisation + Nutrition + Reproduction + Phylogenetic Relationship\n• Five kingdoms = Monera, Protista, Fungi, Plantae, Animalia\nTRICK - “C-B-N-R-P” Criteria\nCell\n Body\n Nutrition\n Reproduction\n Phylogeny.\n→\n→\n→\n→\nTRICK - “M-P-F-P-A” Five Kingdoms\nMonera\n Protista\n Fungi\n Plantae\n Animalia.\n→\n→\n→\n→\nFeature\nMonera\nProtista\nFungi\nPlantae\nAnimalia\nCell\nProkaryotic\nEukaryotic\nEukaryotic\nEukaryotic\nEukaryotic\nWall\nPeptidoglycan\nFew present\nChitin\nCellulose\nAbsent\nNucleus membrane\nAbsent\nPresent\nPresent\nPresent\nPresent\nNutrition\nAuto/Hetero\nAuto/Hetero\nHeterotroph\nAutotroph\nHeterotroph\nTRICK - “Only MONERA = Prokaryotic”\nNuclear membrane absent only in Monera among the five kingdoms.\nWall material: Monera Peptidoglycan | Fungi Chitin | Plantae Cellulose | Animalia None.\nEXAM TRAP -\nFungi wall = Chitin, NOT cellulose. Animalia = no cell wall.","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.1 Introduction to Biology & the Living World\n7. Monera Groups — M-H-T / Cyanobacteria / Mycoplasma\n• Methanogens\n methane producing\n Methanobacterium\n→\n→\n• Halophiles\n saline conditions\n Halococcus\n→\n→\n• Thermoacidophiles\n hot sulphur springs\n Sulfolobus\n→\n→\n• Cyanobacteria\n nitrogen fixing, blue-green autotrophic bacteria\n Nostoc, Anabaena\n→\n→\n• Nostoc & Anabaena fix atmospheric N2 through Heterocysts\n• Mycoplasma\n no cell wall; parasitic or saprophytic\n→\nTRICK - “M-H-T = Methane–Salt–Hot Acid”\nMethanogen = Methane; Halophile = Salt; Thermoacidophile = Hot acidic/sulphur.\nNostoc + Anabaena = Heterocyst = N2 fixation.\nEXAM TRAP -\nMycoplasma = no cell wall.\n8. Symbiosis — Lichen vs Mycorrhiza\n• Lichen = Fungi + Algae\n• Fungi absorbs water/minerals; algae photosynthesizes\n• Lichen = pioneer on bare rocks + SO2 air-pollution indicator + litmus dye source\n• Mycorrhiza = Fungi + roots of higher plants\n• Fungi helps mineral absorption (especially phosphorus) and protection; plant provides shelter/nutrition\n• Examples in source = Pinus and Orchids\nTRICK - “Lichen = F + A; Mycorrhiza = F + Root”\nLichen extras: Rock pioneer + SO2 indicator + Litmus.\nMycorrhiza extras: Phosphorus absorption + Pinus/Orchid.\n9. Virus vs Viroid vs Prion — Composition First\nType\nDiscovery / Key Name\nComposition\nDisease / Note\nVirus\nIvanowsky; TMV crystallized by\nStanley\nDNA or RNA + protein capsid\nObligate parasite; living/non-\nliving link\nViroid\nT.O. Diener\nRNA only; no protein coat\nStunt disease in Chrysanthemum\nPrion\nStanley B. Prusiner\nProtein only; no nucleic acid\nScrapie; CJD; Kuru\nTRICK - “V = Nucleic Acid + Coat | Viroid = RNA | Prion = Protein”\nComposition se teenon ko pehle separate karo.\nTMV: Ivanowsky = discovery; Stanley = crystallization.\nEXAM TRAP -\nViroid = RNA only; Prion = protein only; Virus = nucleic acid + capsid.","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.1 Introduction to Biology & the Living World\n10. Biological Names — Animal Memory Clusters\nCommon Name\nBiological Name\nCommon Name\nBiological Name\nPeacock\nPavo cristatus\nFrog\nRana tigrina\nCat\nFelis catus\nDog\nCanis lupus familiaris\nCow\nBos taurus\nBuffalo\nBubalus bubalis\nGoat\nCapra aegagrus hircus\nTiger\nPanthera tigris\nLion\nPanthera leo\nLeopard\nPanthera pardus\nBear\nUrsus thibetanus\nRabbit\nOryctolagus cuniculus\nElephant\nElephas maximus indicus\nDolphin\nPlatanista gangetica\nTRICK - “Panthera Trio = Tiger–Lion–Leopard”\nTiger = Panthera tigris | Lion = Panthera leo | Leopard = Panthera pardus.\nSame genus Panthera; species changes.\n11. Biological Names — Plant Memory Clusters\nCommon Name\nBiological Name\nCommon Name\nBiological Name\nCarrot weed\nParthenium hysterophorus\nPea\nPisum sativum\nMustard\nBrassica campestris\nFig\nFicus carica\nTomato\nSolanum lycopersicum\nRice\nOryza sativa\nBanyan\nFicus benghalensis\nSugarcane\nSaccharum officinarum\nOnion\nAllium cepa\nAsafoetida (Hing)\nFerula asafoetida\nCotton\nGossypium\nCoffee\nCoffea arabica\nTea\nCamellia sinensis\nTurmeric (Haldi)\nCurcuma longa\nTRICK - “R-W-P-T = Oryza–Triticum–Solanum–Camellia”\nRice = Oryza sativa | Wheat = Triticum aestivum | Potato = Solanum tuberosum | Tea = Camellia sinensis.\nSame-genus plant pair in listed names: Fig/Banyan = Ficus; Tomato/Potato = Solanum.\n12. Five Taxonomic Example Anchors\nOrganism\nFamily\nOrder\nClass\nPhylum / Division\nHuman — Homo sapiens\nHominidae\nPrimates\nMammalia\nChordata\nHouse Fly — Musca\ndomestica\nMuscidae\nDiptera\nInsecta\nArthropoda\nMango — Mangifera\nindica\nAnacardiaceae\nSapindales\nDicotyledonae\nAngiospermae\nWheat — Triticum\naestivum\nPoaceae\nPoales\nMonocotyledonae\nAngiospermae\nPotato — Solanum\ntuberosum\nSolanaceae\nSolanales\nDicotyledonae\nAngiospermae\nTRICK - “Human–Fly–Mango–Wheat–Potato”\nAnimal examples: Human Chordata; House Fly Arthropoda.\nPlant examples: Mango/Wheat/Potato = Angiospermae.","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.1 Introduction to Biology & the Living World\n13. Final Exam Traps — 10 Red Flags\n• Biology term: Lamarck & Treviranus ≠ Father Aristotle\n• Taxonomy term: de Candolle ≠ Father Linnaeus\n• Genus Capital; species small; scientific name italics\n• Herbarium = drying & pressing\n• Fungi wall Chitin | Plants Cellulose | Animalia no wall | Monera Peptidoglycan\n• Only prokaryotic kingdom = Monera\n• Lichen = Fungi + Algae; SO2 indicator\n• Virus = capsid+nucleic acid | Viroid = RNA only | Prion = protein only\n• TMV: Ivanowsky discovery vs Stanley crystallization\n• Panthera genus: tigris / leo / pardus\nEXAM TRAP - “Coined-vs-Father | Wall | RNA/Protein | Same Genus”\nChapter ke maximum MCQ traps inhi four families se bante hain.\nMASTER MEMORY CHAIN — Poora Chapter Ek Saath\nMASTER - 1-Line Revision Chain\nBiology Bios+Logos; Lamarck/Treviranus term, Aristotle father -> B-Z-M: Theophrastus-Aristotle-Leeuwenhoek -> Addison hormone,\nJenner immunity, Mendel genetics, Hooke cell, Galton eugenics, Cuvier fossil -> Taxonomy de Candolle coined / Linnaeus\nfather+binomial; KPCOFGS -> Whittaker1969: C-B-N-R-P, M-P-F-P-A; Monera only prokaryotic; walls\nPeptidoglycan/Chitin/Cellulose/None -> M-H-T + Heterocyst + Mycoplasma no wall -> Lichen F+A/SO2; Mycorrhiza F+root/P -> Virus\nnucleic acid+capsid / Viroid RNA / Prion protein; Ivanowsky discovery vs Stanley crystal -> Panthera trio; Rice Oryza, Wheat\nTriticum, Potato Solanum, Tea Camellia.\nTOP 5: Biology term vs father | Taxonomy coined vs father | Monera only prokaryotic | Fungi=Chitin | Viroid RNA vs Prion\nProtein.","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.2 Plant Kingdom\nPLANT KINGDOM — MEMORY TRICK SHEET\nGeneral Science · Biology · Chapter 2 · Sirf Yaad Rakhne Wali Cheezein — Tricks + Chains\nYe full notes nahi hai — source chapter ke plant features, Cryptogam/Phanerogam split, Algae, Bryophyta, Pteridophyta,\nGymnosperm, Angiosperm aur Monocot-Dicot facts ko exam-recall ke liye compress kiya gaya hai.\n1. Plant Basics — S-C-P\n• Plants = eukaryotic + multicellular + sessile\n• Nutrition mainly photosynthetic autotrophic\n• Cell wall = Cellulosic\n• Plastids present\nTRICK - “S-C-P = Sessile - Cellulose - Plastids”\nSource chapter ke 3 highlighted plant features: Sessile + Cellulosic wall + Plastids.\nEXAM TRAP -\nSource notes ke comparison mein animals mein ye 3 highlighted features absent hain.\n2. Big Split — Cryptogam vs Phanerogam\nGroup\nSeeds\nReproductive Organ\nMain Groups\nCryptogams\nAbsent\nHidden / inconspicuous\nThallophyta, Bryophyta,\nPteridophyta\nPhanerogams\nPresent\nCone or flower\nGymnosperm, Angiosperm\nTRICK - “Crypto = Hidden + No Seed; Phanero = Visible + Seed”\nCryptogams reproduce by spores.\nPhanerogams ke seeds present hote hain.\nTRICK - “T-B-P / G-A”\nCryptogam trio = Thallophyta-Bryophyta-Pteridophyta.\nPhanerogam pair = Gymnosperm-Angiosperm.\nEXAM TRAP -\nGymnosperm = naked seed; Angiosperm = ovary/fruit covered seed.\n3. Five Plant Groups — Generation + Vascular Memory\nGroup\nSeed\nVascular\nTrue Root/Stem/Leaf\nDominant\nThallophyta\nNo\nNo\nNo\nGametophyte\nBryophyta\nNo\nNo\nNo; rhizoids\nGametophyte\nPteridophyta\nNo\nYes\nYes\nSporophyte\nGymnosperm\nNaked\nYes\nYes\nSporophyte\nAngiosperm\nCovered\nYes\nYes\nSporophyte\nTRICK - “T-B = Gameto; P-G-A = Sporo”\nThallophyta + Bryophyta = Gametophyte dominant.\nPteridophyta + Gymnosperm + Angiosperm = Sporophyte dominant.\nTRICK - “Vascular starts at P”\nThallophyta/Bryophyta = no vascular tissue.\nPteridophyta se Xylem + Phloem present.","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.2 Plant Kingdom\n4. Algae Basics — Thallus, Pectin-Cellulose, Pyrenoid, Holdfast\n• Thallophyta/Algae body = undifferentiated thallus\n• Vascular + mechanical tissues absent\n• Mostly aquatic; gametophyte dominant\n• Green algae wall = Outer Pectin + Inner Cellulose\n• Green algae starch bodies = Pyrenoids in chloroplast\n• Laminaria attaches to rocks by Holdfast\nTRICK - “P-C-P-H”\nPectin outer\n Cellulose inner\n Pyrenoid starch\n Holdfast attachment.\n→\n→\n→\n5. Algal Products — A-C-A + C-S\nProduct / Role\nSource\nAlgin\nBrown algae\nCarrageenan\nRed algae\nAgar\nGelidium + Gracilaria (Red)\nProtein / Space Food\nChlorella + Spirulina\nFood\nPorphyra + Sargassum\nTRICK - “Algin Brown; Carrageenan Red; Agar G-G Red”\nAgar = Gelidium + Gracilaria.\nProtein/space food = Chlorella + Spirulina.\nEXAM TRAP -\nAlgin aur Carrageenan dono hydrocolloids hain, but source algae color different hai.\n6. Green-Brown-Red Algae — Pigment + Food\nType\nKey Pigment\nStored Food\nExamples\nGreen\nChl-a,b\nStarch\nChlamydomonas, Volvox,\nSpirogyra\nBrown\nFucoxanthin + Chl-a,c\nMannitol + Laminarin\nFucus, Ectocarpus, Sargassum,\nLaminaria\nRed\nPhycoerythrin + Chl-a,d\nFloridean starch\nPolysiphonia, Porphyra,\nGracilaria\nTRICK - “Brown = FUCO + MANNI-LAMI”\nBrown algae unique pigment = Fucoxanthin.\nStored food = Mannitol + Laminarin.\nTRICK - “Red = PHYCO + FLORIDEAN”\nRed algae unique pigment = Phycoerythrin.\nStored food = Floridean starch.\nEXAM TRAP -\nSpirogyra = freshwater filamentous GREEN algae.","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.2 Plant Kingdom\n7. Bryophyta — Amphibian + Rhizoid + Gametophyte\n• Wet/moist habitat\n• True root, stem, leaves absent; rhizoids present\n• Vascular tissue absent\n• Gametophyte dominant; sporophyte depends on gametophyte\n• Called Amphibians of Plant Kingdom\n• Sexual reproduction = Oogamous\nTRICK - “B = Bheega + Bina Vascular + Big Gametophyte”\nBryophyta ko wet habitat, absent vascular tissue aur gametophyte dominance se lock karo.\nType\nExamples / Memory\nLiverworts\nMarchantia, Riccia\nMosses\nFunaria = Cord Moss; Sphagnum = Bog Moss\nTRICK - “Marchantia Liver; Funaria Cord; Sphagnum Bog”\nSource notes Sphagnum ko peat moss/fuel history se bhi link karte hain.\nEXAM TRAP -\nBryophyta vascular absent; Pteridophyta vascular present.\n8. Pteridophyta — Vascular Cryptogam\n• Source notes call them “Botanical Snakes of Plant Kingdom (Completely Terrestrial)”\n• First land plants with vascular tissue\n• Damp and shady places\n• True root, stem, leaves present\n• Xylem + Phloem present\n• Seeds absent\n• Water necessary for sexual reproduction\n• Sporophyte dominant\n• Male gametes formed in Antheridium\n• Examples = Selaginella, Pteris, Equisetum, Psilotum\nTRICK - “Pteri = Plant body Proper + Pipes Present, but Seed absent”\nProper root/stem/leaf + vascular pipes = Pteridophyta.\nStill cryptogam, so seeds absent.\nEXAM TRAP - “Equisetum = Only Living Sphenopsida”\nSource marks this as a direct MCQ fact.","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.2 Plant Kingdom\n9. Gymnosperm — Naked Seed Pack\n• Gymno = naked; Sperma = seed\n• Seeds naked; ovary, fruit and true flower absent\n• Sporophyte dominant\n• Root, stem and needle-like leaves present\n• Reproductive organ = unisexual cone\n• Endosperm = Haploid (n)\n• Companion cells of phloem and xylem vessels absent\n• Evergreen woody trees/shrubs\n• Examples = Cycas, Pinus, Ginkgo, Gnetum\nTRICK - “GYM = Naked - No Ovary - No Fruit - Cone - n Endosperm”\nFruit absent because ovary absent.\nEXAM TRAP -\nGymnosperm endosperm = Haploid (n), Angiosperm = Triploid (3n).\n10. Angiosperm — D-T-3n\n• Well-developed flowers + covered seeds\n• Fruit/ovary present; seeds/ovules inside\n• Double fertilisation present\n• Triple Fusion present\n• Endosperm = Triploid (3n)\n• Divided into Monocot and Dicot\nTRICK - “D-T-3 = Double fertilisation - Triple fusion - 3n”\nSource chapter ki highlighted Angiosperm uniqueness.\n11. Gymnosperm vs Angiosperm — Fast Contrast\nFeature\nGymnosperm\nAngiosperm\nSeed\nNaked\nCovered\nFlower\nAbsent\nPresent\nFruit\nAbsent\nPresent\nEndosperm\nHaploid (n)\nTriploid (3n)\nDouble fertilisation\nAbsent\nPresent\nCompanion cells\nAbsent\nPresent\nTRICK - “Gym = No Flower/Fruit/Double; Angio = All Present”\nEndosperm contrast: n vs 3n.","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.2 Plant Kingdom\n12. Monocot vs Dicot — 1-3-P-F vs 2-4/5-R-T\nFeature\nMonocot\nDicot\nCotyledon\n1\n2\nRoot\nFibrous / Adventitious\nTap root\nVascular bundles\nScattered\nRing\nLeaf venation\nParallel\nReticulate\nFlower\nMultiples of 3\nMultiples of 4 or 5\nTRICK - “MONO = 1 - 3x - Parallel - Fibrous”\nOne cotyledon, flower multiples of 3, parallel venation, fibrous roots.\nTRICK - “DI = 2 - 4/5x - Reticulate - Tap”\nTwo cotyledons, flower multiples of 4/5, reticulate venation, tap root.\nEXAM TRAP -\nMonocot vascular bundles scattered; Dicot arranged in ring.\n13. Crop Examples — Monocot vs Dicot\nMonocot Examples\nDicot Examples\nCorn / Maize\nRose\nWheat\nChickpea / Chana\nPearl Millet / Bajra\nTomato\nRice, Bamboo, Sugarcane\nMustard, Pea, Bean\nBanana, Onion\nMango, Apple, Potato\nTRICK - “W-C-R = Wheat-Corn-Rice = Monocot”\nRose-Tomato-Mustard = Dicot high-yield examples.","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.2 Plant Kingdom\n14. Final Exam Traps — 10 Red Flags\n• Cryptogam = seed absent; Phanerogam = seed present\n• Gymnosperm = naked seed/no ovary-fruit; Angiosperm = covered seed\n• Thallophyta + Bryophyta = Gametophyte dominant; Pteridophyta + seed plants = Sporophyte dominant\n• Algin Brown; Carrageenan Red; Agar Gelidium + Gracilaria\n• Fucoxanthin Brown; Phycoerythrin Red\n• Bryophyta vascular absent; Pteridophyta vascular present\n• Equisetum = only living representative of Sphenopsida\n• Gymnosperm endosperm n; Angiosperm endosperm 3n\n• Angiosperm = Double fertilisation + Triple fusion + 3n endosperm\n• Monocot = 1/Fibrous/Parallel/3; Dicot = 2/Tap/Reticulate/4-or-5\nEXAM TRAP - “Seed - Generation - Pigment - Vascular - Endosperm - Cotyledon”\nChapter ke maximum confusion points in 6 families se bante hain.\nMASTER MEMORY CHAIN — Poora Chapter Ek Saath\nMASTER - 1-Line Revision Chain\n“Plants SCP = Sessile-Cellulose-Plastids\n Crypto T-B-P no seed vs Phanero G-A seed\n T-B Gametophyte, P-G-A Sporophyte; vascular\n→\n→\nstarts at P\n Algae P-C-P-H = Pectin/Cellulose-Pyrenoid-Holdfast; products Algin Brown, Carrageenan Red, Agar G-G, Chlorella-\n→\nSpirulina protein\n Green Starch; Brown Fucoxanthin + Mannitol/Laminarin; Red Phycoerythrin + Floridean\n Bryophyta\n→\n→\namphibian, rhizoid, no vascular, gametophyte; Marchantia liver, Funaria cord, Sphagnum bog\n Pteridophyta first vascular land\n→\nplant, true R-S-L, seed absent, sporophyte; Equisetum Sphenopsida\n Gymnosperm naked/no ovary-fruit/cone/n endosperm\n\n→\n→\nAngiosperm covered/flower-fruit/D-T-3n\n Monocot 1-3-Parallel-Fibrous-Scattered; Dicot 2-4/5-Reticulate-Tap-Ring; Wheat-Corn-Rice\n→\n\nmono, Rose-Tomato-Mustard dicot.”\nTOP 5: Crypto vs Phanero | T-B Gameto vs P-G-A Sporo | Brown Fucoxanthin vs Red Phycoerythrin | Gym n vs Angio 3n | Mono\n1/3/Parallel vs Dicot 2/4-5/Reticulate.","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.3 Animal World\nANIMAL WORLD — MEMORY TRICK SHEET\nGeneral Science · Biology · Chapter 3 · Sirf Yaad Rakhne Wali Cheezein — Tricks + Chains\nYe full notes nahi hai — source chapter ke Non-Chordata, Chordata, Vertebrata, fishes, tetrapods, excretory organs,\nexamples/scientific names aur class-wise comparison ko exam recall ke liye compress kiya gaya hai.\n1. Animal Basics — E-M-H-M\n• Animals = Eukaryotic + Multicellular + Heterotrophic + generally Motile\n• Cell wall absent\n• Plastids absent\nTRICK - “E-M-H-M = Eukaryotic - Multicellular - Heterotrophic - Motile”\nPlant contrast from source: animal cell mein cellulosic wall aur plastids absent.\n2. Big Split — Notochord Absent vs Present\nDivision\nCore Cue\nMajor Groups\nNon-Chordata\nNotochord absent\nPorifera to Hemichordata\nChordata\nNotochord present at some life stage\nUrochordata, Cephalochordata, Vertebrata\nTRICK - “NO Noto = Non-Chordata; Noto = Chordata”\nChordata ke hallmark 3 = dorsal notochord + dorsal hollow nerve cord + pharyngeal gill slits.\nTRICK - “Herdmania + Amphioxus = Protochordates”\nHerdmania = Urochordata; Amphioxus = Cephalochordata.\nEXAM TRAP -\nHemichordata ko source notes connecting link between Non-chordates and Chordates batate hain.\n3. Non-Chordata Order — 10 Phyla\n• I Porifera\n• II Cnidaria\n• III Ctenophora\n• IV Platyhelminthes\n• V Aschelminthes / Nematoda\n• VI Annelida\n• VII Arthropoda\n• VIII Mollusca\n• IX Echinodermata\n• X Hemichordata\nTRICK - “Po-Cni-Cte-Pla-As-An-Ar-Mo-Ec-He”\n10 phyla ko sound-chain mein bolo; order yaad rahega.\nSize trap: Arthropoda largest, Mollusca second largest.\n4. Porifera - Cnidaria - Ctenophora\nPhylum\nStandout Feature\nExamples\nPorifera\nChoanocytes + canal system + high\nregeneration\nSpongilla, Euspongia, Sycon\nCnidaria\nCnidocytes/Cnidoblasts; Polyp vs Medusa\nHydra, Physalia, Adamsia, Pennatula\nCtenophora\nBioluminescence + 8 comb rows\nCtenoplana","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.3 Animal World\nTRICK - “P-C-C = Collar - Cnidocyte - Comb”\nPorifera = Collar cells.\nCnidaria = Cnidocytes.\nCtenophora = 8 Comb rows + light.\nTRICK - “Polyp Sits, Medusa Floats”\nPolyp = sessile; Medusa = free-floating.\nEXAM TRAP -\nPhysalia = Portuguese man-of-war; Spongilla = freshwater sponge.\n5. Flat - Round - Segmented Worms\nGroup\nBody/Coelom\nExcretion\nExamples\nPlatyhelminthes\nFlat; acoelomate\nFlame cells\nTaenia, Fasciola, Planaria\nAschelminthes\nRound; pseudocoelomate\nRenette cells\nAscaris, Wuchereria,\nAncylostoma\nAnnelida\nSegmented; true coelom\nNephridia\nNereis, Pheretima, Hirudinaria\nTRICK - “Flat-Flame, Round-Renette, Ring-Nephridia”\nBody form ko excretory organ ke saath pair karo.\nTRICK - “Taenia Tapeworm; Wuchereria Filarial; Pheretima Earthworm”\nHigh-yield example identity chain.\nEXAM TRAP -\nHirudinaria = blood-sucking leech; source notes 33 segments highlight karte hain.\n6. Excretion Super-Chain — F-R-N-M-B-P\nPhylum\nExcretory Structure\nPlatyhelminthes\nFlame cells\nAschelminthes\nRenette cells\nAnnelida\nNephridia\nArthropoda\nMalpighian tubules\nMollusca\nBojanus organs\nHemichordata\nProboscis gland\nEXAM TRAP - “F-R-N-M-B-P”\nFlame\n Renette\n Nephridia\n Malpighian\n Bojanus\n Proboscis.\n→\n→\n→\n→\n→\nYe chapter ka sabse important mix-up chain hai.\n7. Arthropoda — Largest Phylum\n• Largest animal phylum; source notes: about two-thirds species are insects\n• Chitinous exoskeleton + open circulatory system\n• Body commonly head + thorax + abdomen\n• Respiration via gills/book gills/book lungs/trachea\n• Statocysts maintain body balance\n• Excretion = Malpighian tubules","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.3 Animal World\nExample\nScientific Name / Note\nHoney bee\nApis indica\nSilkworm\nBombyx mori\nLac insect\nLaccifera lacca\nMosquito vectors\nAnopheles, Culex, Aedes\nLiving fossil\nLimulus - King Crab\nOther\nShrimp, Scorpion, Locusta\nTRICK - “A-B-L = Apis-Bombyx-Laccifera”\nHoney bee = Apis indica; Silkworm = Bombyx mori; Lac insect = Laccifera lacca.\nEXAM TRAP -\nLimulus = King Crab = living fossil.\n8. Mollusca - Echinodermata - Hemichordata\nGroup\nMemory Cue\nExamples\nMollusca\n2nd largest; CaCO3 shell; Ctenidia; Radula;\nBojanus\nPila, Pinctada, Sepia, Octopus\nEchinodermata\nWater vascular system + tube feet; brain\nabsent\nAsterias, Echinus, Cucumaria, Ophiura\nHemichordata\nProboscis + Collar + Trunk; connecting link\nBalanoglossus, Saccoglossus\nTRICK - “Mollusc = C-R-B”\nCtenidia = respiration, Radula = grinding, Bojanus = excretion.\nTRICK - “Echino = Water + Tube Feet + Aristotle”\nSea urchin food grinder = Aristotle’s lantern.\nTRICK - “Hemi = P-C-T + Proboscis Gland”\nBody = Proboscis-Collar-Trunk; excretion = Proboscis gland.\n9. Vertebrata Tree — Jawless to Tetrapods\nBranch\nGroup\nCue\nAgnatha\nCyclostomata\nJawless; sucker-like circular mouth\nGnathostomata\nPisces + Tetrapoda\nJaws present\nPisces\nChondrichthyes + Osteichthyes\nFins\nTetrapoda\nAmphibia + Reptilia + Aves + Mammalia\nFour limbs / two pairs\nTRICK - “A-G = Jawless-Jawed”\nAgnatha = jawless; Gnathostomata = jawed.\nTRICK - “Cyclo examples P-M”\nPetromyzon = Lamprey; Myxine = Hag fish.","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.3 Animal World\n10. Fish — Chondrichthyes vs Osteichthyes\nFeature\nChondrichthyes\nOsteichthyes\nSkeleton\nCartilage\nBone\nGill slits\n5-7 pairs\n4 pairs\nOperculum\nAbsent\nPresent\nAir bladder\nAbsent\nPresent\nHeart\n2 chambered\n2 chambered\nTemperature\nCold\nCold\nTRICK - “Chondro = Cartilage + Cover NO + Air NO”\nOperculum absent + air bladder absent = source ka direct trap.\nTRICK - “Osteo = Bone + Both Present”\nOperculum present + air bladder present.\nFish / Example\nSpecial Identity\nTorpedo\nElectric organ\nTrygon\nPoison sting\nScoliodon\nDog fish\nPristis\nSawfish\nCarcharodon\nWhite shark\nExocoetus\nFlying fish\nHippocampus\nSea horse\nLabeo\nRohu\nClarias\nWalking catfish\nBetta splendens\nFighting fish\nTRICK - “T-T = Torpedo Electric, Trygon Poison”\nExocoetus flies; Hippocampus horse; Clarias walks.\n11. Tetrapoda Quick Comparison\nClass\nHeart\nTemp.\nSpecial\nAmphibia\n3\nCold\nWater+land; mucous skin;\ntadpole\nReptilia\n3 (Croc 4)\nCold\nDry keratin scales; CaCO3 egg\nAves\n4\nWarm\nFeathers; hollow bones;\ntoothless beak\nMammalia\n4\nWarm\nMammary glands + diaphragm;\nmostly viviparous\nTRICK - “3-3-4-4 / Cold-Cold-Warm-Warm”\nAmphibia-Reptilia = 3 chambered, cold.\nAves-Mammalia = 4 chambered, warm.\nEXAM TRAP -\nReptiles generally 3-chambered, but Crocodile = 4-chambered.","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.3 Animal World\n12. Amphibia + Reptilia Examples\nClass\nExamples\nAmphibia\nRana tigrina Frog; Bufo Toad; Hyla Tree frog; Ichthyophis\nBlindworm; Proteus\nReptilia\nNaja naja Cobra; Typhlops Blind snake; Lizard; Chameleon; Tortoise;\nDinosaur extinct reptile\nTRICK - “R-B-H-I-P”\nAmphibia: Rana-Bufo-Hyla-Ichthyophis-Proteus.\nTRICK - “Naja = Cobra; Typhlops = Blind Snake”\nScientific/common-name direct pair.\n13. Aves — EOPKC Flightless Chain\n• 4-chambered heart + warm-blooded\n• Dry skin with feathers\n• Eggs with CaCO3 covering\n• Forelimbs = wings; jaws = toothless beak\n• Hind limbs covered with scales\n• Bones hollow/pneumatic\nTRICK - “EOPKC = Emu - Ostrich - Penguin - Kiwi - Cassowary”\nSource ka ready-made flightless birds mnemonic.\nOstrich = world’s largest bird.\nBird\nScientific Name\nParrot\nPsittacula\nPeacock\nPavo cristatus\nPigeon\nColumba\n14. Mammalia — Mammary + Diaphragm + Monotreme\n• 4-chambered heart + warm-blooded\n• Hair/fur generally present\n• Mammary glands = unique highlighted feature\n• Diaphragm present\n• Mostly viviparous\n• Horns, hoofs, nails as exoskeletal structures\nTRICK - “Mammal = Milk + Diaphragm + Mostly Live Birth”\nMammary gland aur diaphragm ko mammal signature pair banao.\nEXAM TRAP - “P-E = Platypus-Echidna = Egg-laying Mammals”\nDono oviparous monotremes.\nMammal\nScientific Name\nHuman\nHomo sapiens\nBat\nPteropus\nKangaroo\nMacropus\nCamel\nCamelus","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.3 Animal World\n15. Scientific/Common Name Rapid Bank\nName\nRemember As\nApis indica\nHoney bee\nBombyx mori\nSilkworm\nLaccifera lacca\nLac insect\nPila\nApple snail\nPinctada\nPearl oyster\nAsterias\nStarfish\nEchinus\nSea urchin\nPetromyzon\nLamprey\nExocoetus\nFlying fish\nHippocampus\nSea horse\nClarias\nWalking catfish\nRana tigrina\nFrog\nNaja naja\nCobra\nPavo cristatus\nPeacock\nHomo sapiens\nHuman\nTRICK - “Bee-Bombyx-Lac / Pila-Pearl / Asterias-Star / Naja-Cobra / Pavo-Peacock”\nName bank ko paired chunks mein revise karo.\n16. Final Exam Traps — One-Glance\n• Porifera = Choanocytes\n• Ctenophora = Bioluminescence + 8 comb rows\n• Platyhelminthes = Flame cells\n• Arthropoda largest; Mollusca second largest\n• Limulus = living fossil / King Crab\n• Hemichordata = connecting link; Balanoglossus common example\n• Chondrichthyes = no operculum + no air bladder; Osteichthyes = both present\n• Crocodile = reptile with 4-chambered heart\n• Flightless birds = EOPKC\n• Platypus + Echidna = oviparous mammals\nEXAM TRAP - “Cell - Light - Flame - Size - Fossil - Link - Fish - Croc - EOPKC - Monotreme”\nChapter ke 10 red-flag areas isi sequence mein revise karo.\nMASTER MEMORY CHAIN — Poora Chapter Ek Saath\nMASTER - 1-Line Revision Chain\n“Animal E-M-H-M, wall/plastid absent\n NonChordata no notochord vs Chordata N-N-G; Herdmania+Amphioxus protochordates\n\n→\n→\n10 phyla Po-Cni-Cte-Pla-As-An-Ar-Mo-Ec-He\n P-C-C = Collar/Cnidocyte/Comb; Flat-Flame, Round-Renette, Ring-Nephridia\n\n→\n→\nExcretion F-R-N-M-B-P\n Arthropoda largest/chitin/open/Malpighian; Limulus fossil; Apis-Bombyx-Laccifera\n Mollusc C-R-B, Echino\n→\n→\n\nwater/tube feet/Aristotle, Hemi P-C-T/proboscis gland/link\n Vertebrata: Agnatha Cyclo jawless vs Gnathostomata Pisces+Tetrapoda\n→\n Chondro cartilage/no operculum/no air vs Osteo bone/both present; Torpedo electric, Trygon poison, Exocoetus flying,\n→\nHippocampus sea horse, Clarias walking\n Tetrapods 3-3-4-4 and Cold-Cold-Warm-Warm; Croc4\n Aves EOPKC + hollow bones\n\n→\n→\n→\nMammal mammary+diaphragm; Platypus-Echidna oviparous.”\nTOP 5: F-R-N-M-B-P excretion | Arthropoda largest vs Mollusca second | Chondro no/no vs Osteo yes/yes | Crocodile heart 4 |\nPlatypus-Echidna oviparous mammals.","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.4 Cell\nCELL — MEMORY TRICK SHEET\nGeneral Science · Biology · Chapter 4 · Sirf Yaad Rakhne Wali Cheezein — Tricks + Chains\nYe full notes nahi hai — source chapter ke cell discovery, size records, prokaryote/eukaryote, plant/animal comparison, membrane\ntransport, organelles, plastids, cell cycle, mitosis-meiosis aur exam traps ko fast recall format mein compress kiya gaya hai.\n1. Discovery Timeline — H-L-SV\nScientist\nYear\nMemory Fact\nRobert Hooke\n1665\nDead cork cells; coined Cellula\nA. van Leeuwenhoek\n1674\nFirst living cells\nSchleiden & Schwann\n1838-39\nCell Theory\nRudolf Virchow\n1855\nNew cells from pre-existing cells\nTRICK - “Hooke Dead, Leeuwenhoek Live, S-S Theory, Virchow Old-to-New”\nOrder: 1665 > 1674 > 1838-39 > 1855.\nCellula = Hooke; Omnis cellula-e-cellula = Virchow.\nEXAM TRAP -\nHooke = dead cork; Leeuwenhoek = first living cells.\n2. Cell Records — O-M-N\n• Largest cell = Ostrich egg; source note 15 x 13 cm, about 1.4 kg\n• Smallest cell = Mycoplasma PPLO; 0.1 micrometre\n• Longest cell = Neuron; 90-100 cm\n• RBC = Biconcave; about 7.2 micrometre\nTRICK - “O-M-N = Ostrich - Mycoplasma - Neuron”\nLargest > Smallest > Longest ko alag records ki tarah yaad karo.\nEXAM TRAP -\nLargest cell aur longest cell same nahi.\n3. Prokaryote vs Eukaryote — 70/80 + Circle/Linear\nFeature\nProkaryote\nEukaryote\nSize\n0.1-5 micrometre\n10-100 micrometre\nNucleus\nNo true nucleus; nucleoid\nTrue nucleus\nOrganelles\nMembrane-bound absent\nPresent\nRibosome\n70S\n80S cytoplasm; 70S mito/plastid\nDNA\nOne naked circular; no histone\nMultiple linear; histone present\nRespiration\nMesosome - source note\nMitochondria\nTRICK - “Pro = 70 + Circle + No Histone; Eu = 80 + Linear + Histone”\nEukaryote exception: mitochondria/plastids ke ribosomes 70S.\nTRICK - “70 = 50+30; 80 = 60+40”\nRibosome subunit trap ko direct number pair se lock karo.\nEXAM TRAP -\nEukaryotic cytoplasm 80S, but mitochondria/plastids 70S.","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.4 Cell\n4. Plant vs Animal — W-P-V-C\nFeature\nPlant\nAnimal\nCell wall\nPresent - cellulose\nAbsent\nPlastid\nPresent\nAbsent\nVacuole\nLarge central\nVery small/absent\nCentrosome\nAbsent\nPresent\nGolgi\nSmaller; Dictyosomes\nLarger\nMitochondria\nFewer\nMore\nTRICK - “Plant = Wall + Plastid + big Vacuole; Animal = Centrosome”\nPlant Golgi = Dictyosomes.\nEXAM TRAP -\nCentrosome animal; plastid and cell wall plant.\n5. Membrane Transport — D-O-E-E\nProcess\nDirection / Requirement\nHook\nDiffusion\nHigh to Low; no energy\nGas > Liquid > Solid\nOsmosis\nWater across semipermeable membrane\nRoot water absorption\nEndocytosis\nInto cell; energy-dependent\nPhago solid / Pino liquid\nExocytosis\nOut of cell\nCell vomiting\nTRICK - “D-O-E-E = Diffuse - Osmose - Enter - Exit”\nEndocytosis enters; exocytosis exits.\nTRICK - “Phago = Food/Solid; Pino = Pani/Liquid”\nDono Endocytosis ke types.\nEXAM TRAP -\nPhagocytosis solid; Pinocytosis liquid.\n6. Osmosis — Hypo Swell, Iso Same, Hyper Shrink\nSolution\nCondition\nCell Effect\nHypotonic\nOutside concentration < cell\nSwells\nIsotonic\nEqual\nNo change\nHypertonic\nOutside concentration > cell\nShrinks\nTRICK - “HYPO = Hawa Bharo, HYPER = Hatao Pani”\nHypotonic swells; Hypertonic shrinks; Isotonic same.\n• Aquaporins = specialised water-channel proteins responding to osmotic gradient\n7. Cell Boundary + Protoplasm\n• Cell wall = plant only; cellulose; permeable and protective\n• Cell membrane = all cells; very thin, flexible, selectively permeable\n• Source composition = Protein 50% + Lipid 40% + Carbohydrate 10%\n• Protoplasm term coined by J.E. Purkinje, 1839; source notes discovery by Felix Dujardin\n• Huxley called protoplasm the physical basis of life\n• Cytoplasm = protoplasm excluding nucleus","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.4 Cell\n• Nucleoplasm = protoplasm inside nucleus\nTRICK - “50-40-10 = Protein-Lipid-Carb”\nCell membrane composition ka source-number pack.\nTRICK - “Purkinje Protoplasm; Huxley Physical Basis”\nCoiner aur quote ko pair mein yaad karo.\n8. Nucleus — Brown 1831, DNA+Histone, 23 Pairs\n• Nucleus discovered by Robert Brown, 1831\n• Control centre / brain of cell\n• Nuclear membrane = double membrane with pores\n• Nucleolus discovered by Fontana; synthesises r-RNA\n• Chromatin condenses into chromosomes during division\n• Chromosome = DNA + Histone; Gene = functional DNA segment\n• Human = 23 pairs = 22 autosome pairs + 1 sex-chromosome pair\n• Male XY; Female XX\n• Source notes: Safranin used for nucleus staining\nTRICK - “23 = 22 + 1; Male XY, Female XX”\nHuman chromosome pack.\nTRICK - “Brown Nucleus, Fontana Nucleolus”\nNucleus vs nucleolus discoverer trap.\nEXAM TRAP -\nNucleus largest organelle, but large plant vacuole can be exception in source notes.\n9. ER + Ribosome — Rough Protein, Smooth Lipid\n• ER discovered by Keith R. Porter, Albert Claude & Ernest Fullam, 1945\n• RER = ribosomes present; protein/enzyme synthesis\n• SER = ribosomes absent; lipid/steroid synthesis + detoxification\n• Ribosome discovered by G.E. Palade, 1953\n• Ribosome = membrane-less; protein synthesis\n• Polysome = chain of ribosomes attached to m-RNA\nTRICK - “Rough = Ribosome = Protein; Smooth = Steroid/Lipid”\nRER-P, SER-LS.\nTRICK - “Palade 1953 = Protein Factory”\nRibosome discoverer + function pair.\n10. Golgi — 1898 Post Office\n• Discovered by Camillo Golgi, 1898\n• Plant Golgi = Dictyosomes\n• Cis face = convex, toward nucleus\n• Trans face = concave, toward cell membrane\n• Secretion, storage, packaging, processing\n• Forms lysosomes and sperm acrosome\n• Zone of exclusion = cytoplasm around Golgi with no organelles","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.4 Cell\nTRICK - “Cis = Comes from nucleus; Trans = Travels to membrane”\nShape hook: Cis convex, Trans concave.\nTRICK - “Golgi Makes L-A”\nL = Lysosome; A = Acrosome.\nEXAM TRAP -\nGolgi = Post office / Traffic police of cell.\n11. Lysosome + Mitochondria — Suicide vs Power\nOrganelle\nDiscoverer / Year\nCore Memory\nLysosome\nChristian de Duve, 1955\nSuicidal bag; hydrolytic enzymes\nMitochondria\nAlbert von Kolliker, 1857\nPowerhouse; ATP; Cristae\n• Lysosome digests food, foreign particles, damaged organelles, bacteria, viruses and waste\n• Mitochondria = double membrane; inner folds = Cristae\n• ATP = Adenosine Triphosphate; energy currency\n• Mitochondria semi-autonomous: own circular naked DNA, RNA and 70S ribosomes\n• Source notes also link mitochondria with apoptosis\nTRICK - “De Duve Suicide 1955; Kolliker Power 1857”\nLysosome = digestion/self-digestion; Mito = ATP.\nEXAM TRAP -\nCristae = mitochondrial inner folds.\n12. Plastids — C-C-L + A-E-A\nPlastid\nPigment / Colour\nMain Role\nChloroplast\nGreen; chlorophyll\nPhotosynthesis - Kitchen\nChromoplast\nColour except green\nFruit/flower colours\nLeucoplast\nColourless\nStorage\nTRICK - “C-C-L = Chloroplast - Chromoplast - Leucoplast”\nGreen - Colour - Colourless.\nTRICK - “A-E-A = Amyloplast - Elaioplast - Aleuroplast”\nAmyloplast = starch; Elaioplast = oil; Aleuroplast = protein.\n• Source notes credit plastid discovery to Ernst Haeckel and Schimper\n• Plastids = double-membrane, semi-autonomous, plant cells only\nEXAM TRAP -\nChloroplast = Kitchen; Mitochondria = Powerhouse; Lysosome = Suicidal bag; Golgi = Post office.\n13. Vacuole + Centrosome + Peroxisome\n• Vacuole membrane = Tonoplast\n• Vacuole = storage house / dustbin\n• Plant vacuole may occupy 60-90% cell volume; helps turgidity\n• Animal vacuoles very small or absent\n• Centrosome forms poles during animal-cell division","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.4 Cell\n• Centrioles = 9 + 0 arrangement of triplet microtubules\n• Peroxisomes in liver detoxify H2O2 to H2O + O2\nTRICK - “Tono = Vacuole Top Cover”\nTonoplast = vacuole membrane.\nTRICK - “9+0 = Centriole; H2O2 = Peroxisome”\nTwo direct one-liner facts.\n14. Cell Cycle — G1-G0-S-G2-M\nPhase\nMemory Event\nG1\nGrowth; comparatively longest after division\nG0\nQuiescent / non-dividing\nS\nDNA + centrosome replication; histone synthesis\nG2\nPrepare for mitosis; proteins/organelles; DNA check\nM\nKaryokinesis + Cytokinesis\nTRICK - “Grow - Stop - Synthesize DNA - Get Ready - Multiply”\nG1 > G0 > S > G2 > M.\n• Plant-cell cytokinesis = Phragmoplast forms during new cell-wall construction\n15. Mitosis vs Meiosis — 2n Same vs n Half\nFeature\nMitosis\nMeiosis\nCells\nSomatic\nGerm\nDivisions\n1\n2\nChromosome\n2n to 2n\n2n to n\nProducts\n2 identical\n4 haploid, genetically different\nPurpose\nGrowth + repair\nGamete formation + variation\nTRICK - “MITO = Maintain Number; MEIO = Make Half”\nMitosis keeps chromosome number same.\nMeiosis halves it.\nTRICK - “P-M-A-T”\nKaryokinesis stages: Prophase > Metaphase > Anaphase > Telophase.\n• Walther Flemming discovered mitosis by studying chromosomes\n• Uncontrolled mitosis = Cancer\n• Source notes link Turner and Klinefelter syndromes with meiotic chromosome-segregation errors\n• Meiosis-I Prophase-I: Leptotene stage = chromosome condensation\nEXAM TRAP -\nMitosis = 2 identical; Meiosis = 4 haploid/different.\n16. Final Exam Traps — One-Glance\n• Hooke dead cork vs Leeuwenhoek first living cells\n• Largest Ostrich egg; smallest Mycoplasma PPLO; longest Neuron\n• 70S prokaryote + mito/plastid; 80S eukaryotic cytoplasm\n• Centrosome animal; plastid/cell wall plant\n• Phagocytosis solid; Pinocytosis liquid\n• Mito Powerhouse; Lysosome Suicidal; Golgi Post office; Chloroplast Kitchen","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.4 Cell\n• Golgi forms lysosome + sperm acrosome\n• Plant vacuole 60-90%; membrane Tonoplast\n• Mitosis 2n to 2n; Meiosis 2n to n\n• Uncontrolled mitosis = Cancer\nEXAM TRAP - “Dead-Live / O-M-N / 70-80 / Plant-Animal / Phago-Pino / Organelle Nicknames / Division”\nChapter ke major red flags isi chain mein revise karo.\nMASTER MEMORY CHAIN — Poora Chapter Ek Saath\nMASTER - 1-Line Revision Chain\n“Hooke1665 dead Cellula > Leeuwenhoek1674 living > Schleiden-Schwann1838-39 theory > Virchow1855 old-to-new\n records O-M-\n→\nN = Ostrich-Mycoplasma-Neuron\n Pro 70/circular/no histone/mesosome vs Eu 80/linear/histone/mitochondria; mito-plastid 70S\n\n→\n→\nPlant wall+plastid+big vacuole vs Animal centrosome\n transport D-O-E-E; Hypo swell, Iso same, Hyper shrink; Phago solid/Pino\n→\nliquid\n membrane 50-40-10; Purkinje protoplasm/Huxley physical basis\n Brown nucleus1831, Fontana nucleolus, humans\n→\n→\n23=22+1 XY/XX\n RER protein, SER lipid-steroid; Palade ribosome1953\n Golgi1898 Post Office, Cis nucleus/Trans membrane, forms\n→\n→\nLysosome+Acrosome\n De Duve lysosome1955 suicide vs Kolliker mito1857 powerhouse/Cristae/ATP/own DNA-RNA-70S\n plastids\n→\n→\nC-C-L, leucoplast A-E-A; Tonoplast vacuole, centriole9+0, peroxisome H2O2\n cycle G1-G0-S-G2-M\n Mitosis 2n-2n/2 identical vs\n→\n→\nMeiosis 2n-n/4 haploid; PMAT; cancer = uncontrolled mitosis.”\nTOP 5: Hooke dead vs Leeuwenhoek living | 70S/80S exception | Phago solid vs Pino liquid | Golgi makes lysosome+acrosome |\nMitosis same vs Meiosis half.","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.5 Tissue\nTISSUE — MEMORY TRICK SHEET\nGeneral Science · Biology · Chapter 5 · Sirf Yaad Rakhne Wali Cheezein — Tricks + Chains\nYe full notes nahi hai — source chapter ke Plant + Animal tissues, Meristematic/Permanent tissue, Xylem-Phloem, Epidermis-Cork,\nEpithelium, Connective tissue, Muscle aur Nervous tissue ke high-confusion facts ko fast recall format mein compress kiya gaya\nhai.\n1. Tissue Basics — Cell > Tissue > Organ > System\n• Tissue = similar origin/basic structure wale cells ka group jo ek unit ki tarah function karta hai\n• Multicellular organisms mein tissues division of labour create karte hain\n• Organisation level = Cell > Tissue > Organ > Organ System > Animal\n• Plant tissues = Meristematic + Permanent\n• Animal tissues = Epithelial + Connective + Muscular + Nervous\nTRICK - “ECMN = Epithelial - Connective - Muscular - Nervous”\nAnimal tissue ke 4 types ka direct code.\n2. Meristematic Tissue — A-I-L\n• Immature/undifferentiated cells; continuously divide\n• No intercellular spaces, no vacuoles\n• Prominent nucleus + dense cytoplasm + thin cellulose wall\nMeristem\nLocation\nFunction\nApical\nShoot/root tips\nLength / elongation\nIntercalary\nLeaf base, nodes/internodes\nLength growth\nLateral\nSides of stem/root\nThickness / girth\nTRICK - “A-I-L = Apex Length, Inter-node Length, Lateral Girth”\nApical = tip; Intercalary = node/leaf base; Lateral = side.\nEXAM TRAP -\nProtoderm = apical meristem ka root-associated part, as stated in source notes.\nTRICK - “Cork = Secondary Meristem Product”\nSource notes cork ko secondary meristematic tissue batate hain; cork cells dead + suberin.\n3. Simple Permanent Tissue — P-C-S\nTissue\nLiving/Dead\nWall / Space\nMain Role\nParenchyma\nLiving\nThin wall; spaces present\nStorage\nCollenchyma\nLiving\nCorner thickening; spaces\nusually absent\nSupport + flexibility\nSclerenchyma\nDead\nThick lignified wall; spaces\nabsent\nStrength / safety\nTRICK - “P-C Live, S Dead”\nParenchyma + Collenchyma living; Sclerenchyma dead.\nEXAM TRAP - “Coconut Husk = Sclerenchyma”\nDirect source example.","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.5 Tissue\n4. Parenchyma Modifications — Chloro vs Aero\nModification\nFeature\nFunction\nChlorenchyma\nChloroplast present\nPhotosynthesis\nAerenchyma\nLarge air spaces\nBuoyancy in aquatic plants\nTRICK - “Chloro = Chlorophyll; Aero = Air”\nChlorenchyma photosynthesis; Aerenchyma floating.\nEXAM TRAP -\nAerenchyma = buoyancy, not photosynthesis.\n5. Complex Permanent Tissue — Xylem vs Phloem\nTissue\nTransport\nKey Components / Fact\nXylem\nUnidirectional water + minerals\nTracheids, vessels, xylem parenchyma,\nfibres\nPhloem\nBidirectional food\nFood-conducting complex tissue\nTRICK - “Xylem = X-it Upward; Phloem = Food Both Ways”\nSource note: Xylem unidirectional roots > stem > leaves.\nPhloem bidirectional food transport.\nEXAM TRAP -\nXylem parenchyma = living component of xylem.\n6. Protective Tissue — Epidermis + Cork\nFeature\nEpidermis\nCork\nNature\nOutermost single layer\nMultilayered dead cells\nSpecial\nCuticle may form waxy layer\nSuberin in walls\nIntercellular space\nNot highlighted\nAbsent\nExtra\nRoot hairs from root epidermis\nWaterproof protection\nTRICK - “Cork = Dead + Suberin + No Space = Waterproof”\nMechanical injury, infection aur water loss se protection.\nTRICK - “Root Hair = Epidermis Extension”\nWater absorption ke liye surface area badhata hai.\n7. Stomata — Dicot Bean, Monocot Dumbbell\n• Stomata = leaf epidermis ke small pores\n• Functions = transpiration + gaseous exchange\n• Water enters guard cells > turgid > stoma opens\n• Water exits > relaxed/shrunken > stoma closes\n• Dicot guard cells = bean-shaped\n• Monocot guard cells = dumbbell-shaped\nTRICK - “D-B / M-D = Dicot-Bean, Monocot-Dumbbell”\nGuard-cell shape ka direct exam pair.","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.5 Tissue\nEXAM TRAP -\nWater IN = Open; Water OUT = Close.\n8. Epithelial Tissue — SC3 + Stratified\nType\nLocation\nSimple squamous\nBlood vessels, alveoli, mouth, alimentary canal\nStratified squamous\nSkin\nCuboidal\nKidney tubules, sweat/salivary/thyroid glands\nColumnar\nStomach, intestine, gall bladder, bile duct\nCiliated columnar\nTrachea, oviduct, vas deferens\nGlandular single-cell\nGoblet cell\nTRICK - “SC3 = Squamous - Cuboidal - Columnar - Ciliated Columnar”\nSource quick-recall code for simple epithelial forms.\nEXAM TRAP - “Kidney = Cuboidal; Trachea = Ciliated; Skin = Stratified”\n3 highest-yield location traps.\n9. Tendon vs Ligament — M vs B\nTissue\nConnects\nFibre / Flexibility\nTendon\nBone to Muscle\nWhite collagen; strong, less flexible\nLigament\nBone to Bone\nYellow elastin; more flexible\nTRICK - “Tendon = To Muscle; Ligament = Links Bones”\nTendon B-M; Ligament B-B.\nEXAM TRAP -\nCollagen = Tendon; Elastin = Ligament.\n10. Blood & Lymph — 55/45 + RBC Journey\nComponent\nKey Fact\nPlasma\n55%; about 90% water, 8% protein, 2% minerals/ions\nRBC\nFormed bone marrow; destroyed spleen\nWBC\nNeutrophil, Eosinophil, Basophil, Lymphocyte, Monocyte\nPlatelets\nBlood clotting\nLymph\nColourless; RBC absent\nTRICK - “55 Plasma + 45 Cells”\nSource blood ratio.\nTRICK - “RBC: Bone Marrow Birth, Spleen Burial”\nSpleen = graveyard of RBC.\nTRICK - “N-E-B-L-M”\nWBC types: Neutrophil-Eosinophil-Basophil-Lymphocyte-Monocyte.\nEXAM TRAP -\nLymph = colourless + RBC absent.","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.5 Tissue\n11. Loose Connective — Adipose vs Areolar\nTissue\nWhere / Nature\nFunction\nAdipose\nBelow skin, around organs, bone marrow\nFat storage + insulation + shock absorption\nAreolar\nLoose jelly matrix; below skin / spaces / near\nmuscles-vessels\nRepair + filling spaces\nTRICK - “Adipose = Add Fat; Areolar = Area Fill”\nAdipose stores; Areolar repairs/fills.\n12. Bone vs Cartilage — Hard Salts vs Chondrin\nTissue\nMatrix / Nature\nHigh-Yield Facts\nBone\nHard/stiff; CaPO4 + CaCO3\nOsteoblasts = bone-forming;\nsupport/protection\nCartilage\nFlexible; chondrin + sugar + collagen +\nelastin\nJoints, nose tip, outer ear, trachea, throat\nTRICK - “Bone = CaPO4 + CaCO3; Cartilage = Chondrin”\nHard vs flexible ko matrix ke saath pair karo.\nTRICK - “Osteoblast = Builds Bone”\nStem cells source notes mein bone marrow mein present.\nEXAM TRAP -\nBone salt pair ko CaPO4 + CaCO3 se yaad rakho.\n13. Muscle — Striated / Smooth / Cardiac\nFeature\nStriated\nSmooth\nCardiac\nShape\nCylindrical\nSpindle\nBranched cylindrical\nNuclei\nMulti\nUni\nUni\nBands\nPresent\nAbsent\nPresent\nControl\nVoluntary\nInvoluntary\nInvoluntary\nFatigue\nFast\nSlow/resistant\nNo fatigue\nSpecial\nSkeleton attached\nInternal organs\nIntercalated discs\nTRICK - “Bands = Striated + Cardiac; Voluntary = Striated Only”\nSmooth no bands; Cardiac striated but involuntary.\nTRICK - “Cardiac = Branched + Intercalated + No Fatigue”\nHeart muscle ka signature trio.\nEXAM TRAP -\nCardiac striated hote hue bhi involuntary hai.\n14. Muscle Proteins — A-M\n• Muscle fibres = elongated contractile cells\n• Movement by contraction-relaxation\n• Contractile proteins = Actin + Myosin","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.5 Tissue\nTRICK - “A-M = Actin + Myosin = Action of Muscle”\nMuscle movement ka direct protein pair.\n15. Nervous Tissue — Neuron + Neurilemma\n• Functions = sensitivity, excitability, coordination\n• Neuron = structural + functional unit\n• Neuron length source note = up to about 1 m\n• Neurons lack dividing capability\n• Locations = brain, spinal cord, nerves\n• Schwann cells = Neurilemma cells in source notes\nTRICK - “Neuron = Nerve Unit, No Division”\nNeuron structure/function unit + non-dividing.\nEXAM TRAP - “Schwann = Neurilemma”\nSource-specific terminology direct pair.\n16. Additional One-Liners\n• Eosine = luminous red acidic dye\n• Eosine stains cytoplasm, RBC, collagen and muscle fibres\n• Stem cells present in bone marrow\n• Cork cells are dead and contain suberin\n• Robert Hooke observed cork cells and coined “Cellula”\nTRICK - “Eosine = Red Acidic Dye”\nCytoplasm + RBC + collagen + muscle fibres ko stain karta hai.\n17. Final Exam Traps — 12 Red Flags\n• Apical tips/length; Intercalary nodes-leaf base/length; Lateral sides/thickness\n• Parenchyma + Collenchyma living; Sclerenchyma dead\n• Aerenchyma buoyancy; Chlorenchyma photosynthesis\n• Xylem unidirectional water; Phloem bidirectional food\n• Dicot guard = bean; Monocot = dumbbell\n• Cork = dead + suberin + no intercellular space\n• Cuboidal kidney; Ciliated trachea/oviduct/vas deferens; Skin stratified squamous\n• Tendon bone-muscle/collagen; Ligament bone-bone/elastin\n• RBC formed marrow, destroyed spleen\n• Bone = CaPO4 + CaCO3; Cartilage = chondrin\n• Cardiac = striated + involuntary + branched + intercalated discs + no fatigue\n• Neuron does not divide; Schwann = Neurilemma\nEXAM TRAP - “A-I-L / P-C-S / Chloro-Aero / X-P / D-B-M-D / Cork / Epi / T-L / RBC / Bone / Cardiac / Neuron”\nChapter ke 12 most-confusable zones isi sequence mein revise karo.","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.5 Tissue\nMASTER MEMORY CHAIN — Poora Chapter Ek Saath\nMASTER - 1-Line Revision Chain\n“Tissue = similar cells; Plant M+P, Animal ECMN\n Meristem A-I-L: Apical tip-length, Intercalary node-length, Lateral side-girth; no\n→\nvacuole/space\n Simple P-C-S = Parenchyma live/storage, Collenchyma live/flexible, Sclerenchyma dead/strength;\n→\nCoconut=Sclerenchyma; Chloro photosynthesis, Aero buoyancy\n Xylem one-way water, Phloem two-way food, xylem parenchyma\n→\nliving\n Epidermis + Cork; Cork dead+suberin+no space; Root hair epidermis\n Stomata: Dicot bean, Monocot dumbbell; water-in\n→\n→\nopen, water-out close\n Epithelial SC3; kidney cuboidal, trachea ciliated, skin stratified\n Tendon B-M collagen vs Ligament B-B\n→\n→\nelastin\n Blood 55 plasma/45 cells; RBC marrow birth-spleen burial; WBC N-E-B-L-M; lymph colourless/no RBC\n Adipose fat,\n→\n→\nAreolar fill; Bone CaPO4+CaCO3/Osteoblast vs Cartilage Chondrin\n Muscle Actin+Myosin; bands Striated+Cardiac, voluntary\n→\nStriated only; Cardiac branched/intercalated/no fatigue\n Neuron functional unit/no division; Schwann=Neurilemma; Eosine red\n→\nacidic dye.”\nTOP 5: A-I-L meristems | P-C living vs S dead | Xylem one-way vs Phloem two-way | Tendon B-M vs Ligament B-B | Cardiac striated\nbut involuntary.","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.6 Biological Process\nBIOLOGICAL PROCESS — MEMORY TRICK SHEET\nGeneral Science · Biology · Chapter 6 · Nutrition + Digestion + Respiration + Blood + Excretion + Plant Transport\nYe full notes nahi hai — source chapter ke high-memory facts, numbers, enzyme-location pairs, vitamin deficiencies, heart/blood\nexceptions, nephron path aur plant transport ko exam-recall tricks mein compress kiya gaya hai.\n1. Life Processes — N-R-E-T\nProcess\nMeaning\nNutrition\nGetting nutrients from food\nRespiration\nEnergy release from food\nExcretion\nRemoval of metabolic wastes\nTransportation\nMoving substances in body\nTRICK - “N-R-E-T = Nutrition - Respiration - Excretion - Transport”\nBiological process chapter ka master four-process code.\n2. Plant Nutrition — Auto + Special Modes\n• Autotrophic nutrition = inorganic substances se organic food banana\n• Photosynthesis chlorophyll ki presence mein hota hai\n• Source equation: 6CO2 + 12H2O + sunlight > C6H12O6 + 6O2 + 6H2O\n• Plant macronutrients listed = C, H, O, N, P, K, Ca, Mg, S\n• Micronutrients listed = Fe, B, Zn, Mn, Cu, Mo, Cl\nTRICK - “Auto = Apna Food; Hetero = Har Baar Bahar”\nAutotroph khud food banata; heterotroph organic source par depend karta.\nSpecial Mode\nExamples / Hook\nInsectivorous\nNepenthes, Drosera, Venus flytrap - N deficient soil\nParasitic\nCuscuta - complete stem parasite; sucker roots\nSymbiotic\nLegume root nodules + Rhizobium - N fixing\nTRICK - “N-D-V / Cuscuta Sucker / Rhizobium Root-N”\nInsectivorous = Nepenthes-Drosera-Venus.\nCuscuta parasite; Rhizobium symbiotic N-fixer.\nEXAM TRAP -\nCuscuta = parasite; Rhizobium = symbiont, parasite nahi.\n3. Heterotrophic Modes — H-S-P\nMode\nProcess\nExamples\nHolozoic\nIngest > Digest > Absorb\nHuman, Amoeba, Goat, Lion\nSaprophytic\nDead/decayed matter ka extracellular\ndigestion\nRhizopus, Yeast, Penicillium\nParasitic\nHost ke inside/outside\nTicks, Lice, Leech, Tapeworm, pathogenic\nbacteria\nTRICK - “H-S-P = Holozoic - Saprophyte - Parasite”\nHolo = ingest; Sapro = dead matter; Para = host.\n4. Energy Values — 9.3 > 4.3 > 4.1\nNutrient\nEnergy / g\nHigh-Yield Fact\nFat\n9.3 Kcal\nHighest energy","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.6 Biological Process\nProtein\n4.3 Kcal\nDeficiency: Marasmus + Kwashiorkor\nCarbohydrate\n4.1 Kcal\nC:H:O = 1:2:1\nTRICK - “F-P-C = 9.3 - 4.3 - 4.1”\nFat > Protein > Carbohydrate energy order.\nTRICK - “Protein kam = M-K”\nM = Marasmus; K = Kwashiorkor.\n• Term “Vitamin” coined by Casimir Funk\n• Body water in source notes = about 65-70%\n• Essential amino acids = His, Ile, Leu, Lys, Met, Phe, Thr, Trp, Val\nTRICK - “HILL-MPTTV”\nEssential amino acids mnemonic chunk: His-Ile-Leu-Lys / Met-Phe-Thr-Trp-Val.\n5. Vitamins — ADEK vs B+C\nTRICK - “ADEK = Fat Soluble; B+C = Water Soluble”\nSolubility ka biggest vitamin trap.\nVitamin\nChemical Name\nDeficiency / Effect\nA\nRetinol / beta-Carotene\nNight blindness, xerophthalmia\nD\nCalciferol\nRickets child; osteoporosis adult\nE\nTocopherol\nSterility/infertility; weak muscles\nK\nNaphthoquinone/Phylloquinone\nBleeding due to clot failure\nTRICK - “A-Night, D-Rickets, E-Sterility, K-Klotting”\nADEK ko deficiency/function se pair karo.\nVitamin\nName\nDeficiency\nB1\nThiamine\nBeri-Beri\nB2\nRiboflavin\nCheilosis\nB3\nNiacin\nPellagra - 3D\nB5\nPantothenic acid\nSkin/growth/hair/fertility effects\nB6\nPyridoxine\nAnaemia, skin disease, cramps\nB7\nBiotin\nSkin disease, hair loss\nB9\nFolic acid\nAnaemia, growth, neural-tube defects\nB12\nCobalamin\nAnaemia + nervous/RBC/DNA effects\nC\nAscorbic acid\nScurvy\nEXAM TRAP - “B1 Beri, B3 3D, C Scurvy, D Rickets, K Klotting”\nB3 Pellagra = Dermatitis + Diarrhoea + Dementia.\n6. Digestion Process — I-D-A-A-D\n• Ingestion > Digestion > Absorption > Assimilation > Defecation\n• Alimentary canal length in source notes = 8-10 m\nTRICK - “I-D-A-A-D”\nFood journey ka 5-step process.\nPart\nSource Length / pH\nCore Function\nPharynx\n12 cm\nFood-air common passage; epiglottis\nprotects trachea\nOesophagus\n22-24 cm\nPeristalsis\nStomach\n25 cm; pH 2.0\nProtein digestion + chyme","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.6 Biological Process\nSmall intestine\n25 cm + 2.4 m + 3.5 m\nComplete digestion + microvilli absorption\nLarge intestine\nCaecum 6 cm + Colon 1.3 m + Rectum 12-15\ncm\nWater absorption + faeces\nTRICK - “Stomach 2; Small Complete; Large Water”\npH 2 stomach, complete digestion small intestine, water absorption large intestine.\n7. Teeth — 2-1-2-3 = 32\n• Adult dental formula per half jaw = 2 incisors, 1 canine, 2 premolars, 3 molars\n• Total adult teeth = 32\n• Child formula = 2,1,0,2\n• Wisdom tooth = third molar\n• Enamel = outer layer and hardest; Dentine = inner layer\nTRICK - “2-1-2-3 x 4 = 32”\nAdult formula ka direct arithmetic trick.\nTongue Zone - Source Note\nTaste\nFront\nSweet\nFront-side\nSalty\nSide\nSour\nBack\nBitter\n8. Digestive Enzymes — Mouth-Stomach-Pancreas-Intestine\nLocation\nKey Enzymes\nActs On\nMouth\nPtyalin / Amylase\nStarch\nStomach\nPepsin, Rennin, Gastric lipase\nProtein, milk protein, fat\nPancreas\nTrypsin, Chymotrypsin, Amylopsin,\nCarboxypeptidase, Lipase\nProtein/starch/polypeptide/fat\nIntestine\nEnterokinase, Erepsine, Maltase, Sucrase,\nLactase, Lipase, Nuclease\nFinal digestion targets\nTRICK - “Mouth A; Stomach P-R-L; Pancreas T-C-A-C-L”\nMouth = Amylase.\nStomach = Pepsin-Rennin-Lipase.\nPancreas = Trypsin-Chymotrypsin-Amylopsin-Carboxypeptidase-Lipase.\nEXAM TRAP -\nSalivary Ptyalin/Amylase acts on starch.\n9. Liver + Pancreas — Largest vs Second Largest\n• Liver = largest gland\n• Kupffer cells = macrophages\n• Bile emulsifies fat; source pH 7.4-8.5\n• Bile stored in gall bladder\n• Bile pigments = bilirubin + biliverdin\n• Liver functions include glycogenesis, detoxification, urea formation, storage of A,D,E,K\n• Pancreas = second largest gland; mixed gland; pancreatic juice\n• Salivary glands = 3 pairs\n• Source note states: Gall stones = Calcium oxalate\nTRICK - “Liver Largest; Pancreas 2nd + Mixed”\nLargest-vs-second-largest direct trap.","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.6 Biological Process\nTRICK - “B-B = Bilirubin + Biliverdin; Bile in Gall Bladder”\nPigments + storage ek saath yaad karo.\n10. Respiration — 36 vs 2 ATP\nFeature\nAerobic\nAnaerobic\nO2\nRequired\nNot required\nBreakdown\nComplete\nPartial\nEnergy\n36 ATP\n2 ATP\nLocation\nCytoplasm + mitochondria\nCytoplasm only\nProducts\nCO2 + H2O\nEthanol + CO2 in yeast\nExamples\nMost animals/plants\nYeast, bacteria, exercising human muscle\nTRICK - “AERO = Air + 36; ANAERO = Absent Air + 2”\nEnergy difference is the key exam number.\nEXAM TRAP -\nHuman muscles during exercise = anaerobic; yeast fermentation = anaerobic.\n11. Human Respiratory System — 3 Right, 2 Left\nPart\nHigh-Yield Fact\nNostrils\nMucus + hairs clean/moisten air\nPharynx\nCommon passage; epiglottis\nLarynx\nSound; Adam’s apple in men\nTrachea\n10-12 cm; C-shaped hyaline cartilage rings\nRight lung\nLarge/broad; 3 lobes + 3 bronchi\nLeft lung\nSmall/thin; 2 lobes + 2 bronchi\nAlveoli\nBalloon-shaped, single-layered; gas exchange\nTRICK - “Right 3, Left 2; Alveoli = Exchange”\nLung lobes ka direct pair.\nPhase\nO2\nCO2\nDiaphragm\nInspiration\n21%\n0.03%\nFlattens downward\nExhalation\n16%\n4.4%\nDome-shaped\nTRICK - “Inhale Flat; Exhale Dome”\nDiaphragm shape trick.\n12. Gas Transport — O2 97; CO2 70-23-7\n• O2 transport = 97% by haemoglobin\n• CO2 = 70% bicarbonate + 23% haemoglobin + 7% plasma\nTRICK - “O2 = 97 Hb; CO2 = 70-23-7”\nGas-transport number pack.\n13. Respiratory Surfaces — Membrane-Skin-Gill-Trachea-Lung\nOrganism\nSurface\nAmoeba & Planaria\nCell membrane\nEarthworm & Leech\nMoist skin\nFish, Prawns, Tadpole\nGills\nFrog\nMoist skin + lungs","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.6 Biological Process\nMosquito & Housefly\nTrachea + spiracles\nBirds/Lizards/terrestrial animals\nLungs\nTRICK - “Amoeba Membrane, Worm Skin, Fish Gill, Insect Trachea, Land Lung”\nFrog exception = moist skin + lungs.\nEXAM TRAP -\nInsects = trachea + spiracles, not gills.\n14. Blood — 55/45, pH 7.3-7.5\n• Blood pH = 7.3-7.5, alkaline\n• Adult blood = 5-6 L, about 7-8% body weight\n• Plasma = 55%; cells = 45%\nTRICK - “Blood = 55 Liquid + 45 Cells”\nPlasma-cell composition split.\nCell\nCount / Lifespan\nFormation / Role\nRBC\n4.5-5 million/mm3; 120 days\nBone marrow after birth; gas transport\nWBC\n6,000-8,000/mm3; hours-months\nImmunity\nPlatelets\n1.5-3.5 lakh/mm3; 8-10 days\nMegakaryocytes; clotting\nTRICK - “RBC 120; Platelet 8-10; Neutrophil 60-65”\nBlood-cell exam numbers.\nTRICK - “RBC Birth = Marrow; Graveyard = Spleen”\nFormation-destruction pair from source.\n15. WBC Types — N-E-B / L-M\n• Granular = Neutrophils 60-65%, Eosinophils 2-3%, Basophils 0.5-1%\n• Agranular = Lymphocytes 20-25%, Monocytes 2-10%\n• Neutrophils = most abundant WBC\nTRICK - “Granular N-E-B; Agranular L-M”\nN-E-B / L-M classification.\n16. Blood Groups — L / Castello-Sturli / Rh\n• A, B, O discovered by Landsteiner\n• AB discovered by Von de Castello & Sturli, 1902\n• Source notes: O = Universal Donor; AB = Universal Recipient\n• Rh factor discovered by Landsteiner & Wiener, 1940 in Rhesus monkey\n• Source notes: Rh+ = 85% world, 97% India\nTRICK - “Landsteiner ABO; C-S AB; L-W Rh 1940”\nDiscoverer chain ko 3 blocks mein yaad karo.\nEXAM TRAP -\nSource note uses O = universal donor and AB = universal recipient.\n17. Heart — 4 Chambers + Valve Side Trick\n• Heart = 4 chambered: 2 atria + 2 ventricles","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.6 Biological Process\n• Atria receive blood; ventricles pump\n• Double-walled pericardium covers heart\n• Source weights: men 280-340 g; women 230-240 g; newborn 20 g\nFeature\nFact\nTricuspid\nRight atrium - right ventricle\nBicuspid / Mitral\nLeft atrium - left ventricle\nSemilunar\nVentricle - artery\nLub\nClosure of AV valves\nDup\nClosure of semilunar valves\nHeart rate\n72-75 beats/min\nOne beat\n0.85 s\nCardiac output\n5 L/min\nBlood pressure\n120/80 mmHg\nPacemaker\nSA Node\nTRICK - “TRI = Right; BI/Mitral = Left”\nTricuspid right, bicuspid/mitral left.\nTRICK - “Lub AV; Dup Semi”\nHeart sound valve-closure trick.\nTRICK - “72-75 / 0.85 / 5 L / 120-80 / SA”\nHeart number pack.\n18. Blood Flow — Right Deoxy, Left Oxy\nChamber\nFlow\nRight atrium\nDeoxygenated blood from vena cava\nRight ventricle\nDeoxygenated to lungs via pulmonary artery\nLeft atrium\nOxygenated from lungs via pulmonary veins\nLeft ventricle\nOxygenated to body via aorta\nTRICK - “RA Receive Deoxy; RV to Lungs; LA Receive Oxy; LV to Body”\nHeart circulation ko R-R-L-L chain mein bolo.\nEXAM TRAP - “Pulmonary Exception = Artery Deoxy, Vein Oxy”\nMajor vessel exception.\n19. Artery vs Vein vs Capillary\nVessel\nDirection / Blood\nWall / Valve\nArtery\nAway; usually oxygenated\nThick; no valves\nVein\nToward; usually deoxygenated\nThin; valves present\nCapillary\nConnect arteries-veins\nExchange surface\nTRICK - “A = Away; V = Visits Heart”\nPulmonary vessels ko exception ke roop mein separate yaad karo.\n• Varicose veins = dilated superficial veins due to incompetent venous valves, commonly legs\n20. Excretion — Plant + Human\nPlant Waste\nMethod\nLiquid\nTranspiration via stomata\nGases\nStomata + lenticels\nSolid\nVacuoles/dead tissues; leaf shedding","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.6 Biological Process\nUseful waste\nOils, perfumes, gums, resins, rubber, tannin\nTRICK - “Plant Waste = Stoma-Lenticel-Vacuole-Shedding”\nPlant excretion ke routes.\n• Human nitrogenous waste mainly urea; urea produced in liver and removed by kidneys\n• Kidneys = one pair, bean-shaped\n• About 10 lakh nephrons per kidney\n• Nephron = structural + functional unit\n• Urine pH about 6.0, slightly acidic\n• Urine source composition = water 95% + urea 2%\n• Urine colour = urochrome/urobilin\nTRICK - “Liver Makes Urea; Kidney Removes Urea”\nFormation vs excretion organ trap.\n21. Urinary Path + Nephron Path\nOrgan\nRole\nKidney\nForms urine\nUreter\nKidney > bladder\nBladder\nStores urine\nUrethra\nRemoves urine\nTRICK - “K-U-B-U = Kidney-Ureter-Bladder-Urethra”\nUrinary tract order.\nTRICK - “B-P-H-D-C”\nBowman’s capsule > PCT > Henle loop > DCT > Collecting duct.\nTRICK - “B-P-H-D-C > Ureter > Bladder > Urethra”\nSource chapter ka complete nephron-to-exit chain.\nEXAM TRAP -\nNephron = structural & functional unit; about 10 lakh per kidney.\n22. Plant Transport — Xylem vs Phloem\nTransport\nTissue / Material\nDirection\nMechanism\nWater\nXylem; water + minerals\nUpward / unidirectional\nCohesion + adhesion +\ntranspiration pull\nFood\nPhloem; sucrose\nBi-/multidirectional\nMass flow - Munch hypothesis\nTRICK - “Xylem = Water Up; Phloem = Food Both”\nX = xylem-water; P = phloem-food.\nTRICK - “Munch = Mass Flow”\nFood translocation hypothesis.\nEXAM TRAP -\nXylem unidirectional; phloem bidirectional/multidirectional.","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.6 Biological Process\n23. Final Exam Traps — 14 Red Flags\n• Cuscuta parasite vs Rhizobium symbiotic N-fixer\n• Fat 9.3 > Protein 4.3 > Carbohydrate 4.1 Kcal/g\n• Protein deficiency = Marasmus + Kwashiorkor\n• ADEK fat-soluble; B-complex + C water-soluble\n• B1 Beri-Beri; B3 Pellagra; C Scurvy; D Rickets; K bleeding\n• Liver largest gland; pancreas second largest + mixed\n• Aerobic 36 ATP vs anaerobic 2 ATP\n• Frog skin+lungs; insects trachea+spiracles\n• RBC 120 days; platelets 8-10 days; neutrophils most abundant\n• Source notes: O universal donor; AB universal recipient\n• Tricuspid right; bicuspid/mitral left; semilunar ventricle-artery\n• Pulmonary artery deoxygenated; pulmonary vein oxygenated\n• Nephron structural-functional unit; urine pH about 6\n• Xylem water one-way; phloem food both ways\nEXAM TRAP - “Nutrition-Energy-Vitamin-Glands-ATP-Gas-Blood-Valves-Pulmonary-Kidney-X/P”\nExam ke major confusion zones isi order mein revise karo.\nMASTER MEMORY CHAIN — Poora Chapter Ek Saath\nMASTER - 1-Line Revision Chain\n“NRET life processes\n Auto chlorophyll/photosynthesis; N-D-V insectivorous, Cuscuta sucker parasite, Rhizobium root-N symbiotic;\n→\nhetero H-S-P\n energy F-P-C 9.3-4.3-4.1; protein M-K; Vitamin Casimir Funk; ADEK fat vs B+C water; B1 Beri, B3 3D Pellagra, C\n→\nScurvy, D Rickets, K Klotting\n digestion I-D-A-A-D; canal 8-10m; teeth 2-1-2-3x4=32; Mouth Amylase, Stomach P-R-L, Pancreas T-C-A-\n→\nC-L; Liver largest/bile in gall bladder, Pancreas 2nd mixed\n respiration Aero36 vs Anaero2; Right lung3/Left2; inhale-flat/exhale-\n→\ndome; O2=97Hb, CO2=70-23-7; Frog skin+lung, insect trachea\n blood pH7.3-7.5, 55 plasma/45 cells; RBC120 marrow-birth/spleen-\n→\ngrave; platelets8-10 megakaryocyte; WBC N-E-B/L-M\n Landsteiner ABO, Castello-Sturli AB, L-W Rh1940\n heart4; TRI-right, BI-left,\n→\n→\nLubAV/DupSemi, SA node, 120/80\n RA deoxy in, RV pulmonary artery out; LA oxy in, LV aorta out; pulmonary artery deoxy/vein\n→\noxy\n excretion Liver makes urea/Kidney removes; K-U-B-U; nephron B-P-H-D-C; urine pH6\n plants: Xylem water-up by\n→\n→\ntranspiration pull; Phloem sucrose both-way by Munch mass flow.”\nTOP 5: Fat 9.3 > Protein 4.3 > Carb 4.1 | ADEK fat-soluble | Aero36 vs Anaero2 | Pulmonary artery deoxy / vein oxy | Xylem one-\nway water vs Phloem both-way food.","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.7 Control, Coordination & Movement\nCONTROL, COORDINATION & MOVEMENT — MEMORY TRICK\nSHEET\nGeneral Science · Biology · Chapter 7 · Nervous System + Hormones + Muscles + Skeleton + Joints + Plant Coordination\nYe full notes nahi hai — source chapter ke impulse pathway, brain-function pairs, endocrine hormones, muscle/skeleton\nsuperlatives, joint examples aur plant movement/hormone traps ko fast exam recall ke liye compress kiya gaya hai.\n1. Nervous System — C-P-A\nDivision\nComponents / Role\nCNS\nBrain + Spinal cord\nPNS\n12 pairs cranial nerves + 31 pairs spinal nerves\nANS\nSympathetic + Parasympathetic\nTRICK - “C-P-A = Central - Peripheral - Autonomic”\nPNS number pack = 12 cranial + 31 spinal pairs.\nANS pair = Sympathetic + Parasympathetic.\n2. Impulse Path — S-R-S-C-M-E\n• Stimulus > Receptor > Sensory neuron > Brain/Spinal cord > Motor neuron > Effector organ\n• Reflex action = automatic + involuntary response\n• Source notes associate reflex control with spinal cord\nTRICK - “S-R-S-C-M-E”\nStimulus-Receptor-Sensory-CNS-Motor-Effector.\nEXAM TRAP -\nReflex = automatic/involuntary; source notes mein spinal cord se associated, brain se nahi.\n3. Sensory Receptors + Eye\nOrgan\nFunction\nEar\nHearing + body balance\nEye\nVision\nSkin\nTouch + heat + pain\nNose\nSmell\nTongue\nTaste buds\nEye Part\nFact\nIris\nControls pupil expansion/contraction\nCornea\nTransparent front part\nLacrimal gland\nReleases tears\nSuperior rectus\nControls eye movement\nTRICK - “I-C-L-S = Iris-Cornea-Lacrimal-Superior Rectus”\nIris pupil, Cornea transparent, Lacrimal tears, Superior rectus movement.\nEXAM TRAP -\nNervous coordination = electrical impulses; endocrine coordination = chemical hormones.\n4. Brain — Fore-Mid-Hind\n• Brain weight in source notes = about 1300-1400 g","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.7 Control, Coordination & Movement\n• Forebrain = higher functions + voluntary actions\n• Midbrain = visual + auditory reflexes\n• Hindbrain = automatic functions + spinal coordination\nTRICK - “FORE Thinks, MID Reflexes, HIND Runs Basics”\nFore = higher functions; Mid = light/sound reflex; Hind = automatic coordination.\n5. Forebrain — O-C-E-H\nPart\nFunction\nOlfactory lobe\nSmell\nCerebrum\nMemory, consciousness, reasoning, learning\nEpithalamus\nPain, sensation, heat/cold perception\nHypothalamus\nAppetite, thirst, affection, disgust, sleep, temperature\nTRICK - “O-C-E-H”\nOlfactory-Smell, Cerebrum-Memory, Epithalamus-Sensation, Hypothalamus-Home regulation.\nEXAM TRAP - “Cerebrum = Memory; Cerebellum = Balance”\nNaam similar, function opposite trap.\n6. Hindbrain — P-C-M\nPart\nFunction\nPons\nRespiratory centre\nCerebellum\nMuscular balance + posture\nMedulla oblongata\nBreathing rhythm + heart rate + hiccups + ANS\nTRICK - “P-C-M = Pons Respire, Cerebellum Balance, Medulla Heart/Breath”\nHindbrain ke 3 direct function pairs.\nEXAM TRAP -\nMedulla = breathing + heart rate; Hypothalamus = appetite/thirst/sleep/temp.\n7. Endocrine Glands — Hormone-Disease Pairs\nGland / Cell\nHormone\nMain Function / Deficiency\nAnterior Pituitary\nGH / Somatotropic\nGrowth; deficiency = dwarfism/stunted\ngrowth\nThyroid\nThyroxine\nBMR; deficiency linked with cretinism/goitre\nAdrenal\nAdrenaline / Nor-adrenaline\nRaises BP/heartbeat; source calls adrenaline\nDo or Die\nPancreas beta-cells\nInsulin\nLowers blood sugar; deficiency = diabetes\nPancreas alpha-cells\nGlucagon\nRaises blood sugar\nTestes\nTestosterone / Androgen\nMale secondary characters +\nspermatogenesis\nOvaries\nOestrogen + Progesterone\nFemale secondary characters, oogenesis,\nuterus prep\nTRICK - “Beta Insulin DOWN; Alpha Glucagon UP”\nbeta-cells lower blood sugar; alpha-cells raise blood sugar.\nTRICK - “GH-Dwarf | Thyroid-Goitre/Cretinism | Insulin-Diabetes”\nDisease-hormone fast pair.","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.7 Control, Coordination & Movement\nEXAM TRAP - “Adrenaline = Do or Die”\nSource ka direct one-liner.\n8. Muscles — V-I-I + Superlatives\nFeature\nStriated/Skeletal\nSmooth\nCardiac\nControl\nVoluntary\nInvoluntary\nInvoluntary\nBands\nPresent\nAbsent\nPresent\nLocation\nSkeleton\nUterus/intestine/pupil/trachea\nHeart\nTRICK - “V-I-I; Bands = Skeletal + Cardiac”\nStriated voluntary; Smooth involuntary; Cardiac involuntary but striated.\nTRICK - “Masseter Strongest; Gluteus Maximus Largest”\nMuscle superlatives.\nEXAM TRAP -\nCardiac muscle striated hote hue bhi involuntary hai.\n9. Skeleton — 206 + Head 8-14-6-1\n• Total adult bones = 206\n• Cranium = 8\n• Face = 14\n• Ear ossicles = 6\n• Hyoid = 1\nTRICK - “Head = 8 + 14 + 6 + 1”\nCranium-Face-Ear-Hyoid.\nTRICK - “Ear = M-I-S; Stapes Smallest”\nMalleus-Incus-Stapes; Stapes = smallest bone.\n10. Vertebral Column — 7-12-5-1-1\nRegion\nAdult Count\nCervical\n7\nThoracic\n12\nLumbar\n5\nSacrum\n1\nCoccyx\n1\nTRICK - “7-12-5-1-1 = 26 Adult Vertebral Bones”\nNeck-Chest-Waist-Sacrum-Coccyx.\nEXAM TRAP -\nSource notes mention sacrum 5 and coccyx 4 in infants, but adult table counts them as 1 each.\n11. Skeleton Superlatives + Limb Numbers\n• Largest & strongest bone = Femur\n• Smallest bone = Stapes\n• Ribs = 24 = 12 pairs","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.7 Control, Coordination & Movement\n• Sternum = 1\n• Pectoral girdle = Scapula 2 + Clavicle 2 = 4\n• Pelvic girdle = Innominate 2\n• Upper arm = Humerus 2; Lower arm = Radius 2 + Ulna 2\n• Wrist/Carpals = 16\n• Palm/Fingers = Metacarpals 10 + Phalanges 28\n• Lower limb: Femur 2; Tibia 2 + Fibula 2; Patella 2; Tarsal 14 + Metatarsals 10; toe phalanges 28\nTRICK - “Femur Biggest-Strongest; Stapes Smallest; Ribs 24”\n3 skeletal super-facts.\n12. Joints — S-A-E-S\nJoint Type\nMobility\nSource Example\nImmovable\nNo movement\nSkull bones; teeth & jaw joint\nSemi-movable\nLimited\nPubic symphysis / pelvic girdle\nBall & Socket\nAll directions\nShoulder; source table also lists ankles/feet\nHinge\nOne-direction\nElbow, fingertips\nPivotal\nRotation\nAtlas-Axis neck\nSaddle\nTwo-direction\nSource table: wrist & ankle\nTRICK - “Skull None - Atlas Axis Pivot - Elbow Hinge - Shoulder Ball”\nMost-tested examples ka four-point chain.\nTRICK - “Synovial = Smooth Movement”\nMovable joints mein synovial cavity/fluid lubrication.\nEXAM TRAP -\nBall & socket maximum movement; hinge mainly flexion/extension.\n13. Plant Movement — Tropic vs Nastic\nMovement\nNature\nExamples\nTropic\nGrowth-related; direction matters\nPhoto, Geo, Hydro, Chemo\nNastic\nNon-growth; direction-independent\nTouch-me-not / Mimosa; thigmonasty\nTRICK - “Tropic = Turns Toward/Away; Nastic = No Direction”\nTropic mein direction matters; nastic mein direction independent.\nTRICK - “P-G-H-C”\nPhototropism-Light, Geotropism-Gravity, Hydrotropism-Water, Chemotropism-Chemical.\n• Source note: phototropism root = negative / away from light; sunflower example\n• Geotropism: roots positive, shoots negative\n• Hydrotropism: movement towards water\n• Chemotropism: pollen tube grows towards ovule\nEXAM TRAP -\nTouch-me-not = nastic/thigmonasty, tropic nahi.","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.7 Control, Coordination & Movement\n14. Plant Hormones — AGC vs AE\nCategory\nHormone\nKey Function\nGrowth promoter\nAuxin\nApical dominance + cell elongation +\nparthenogenesis\nGrowth promoter\nGibberellin\nBolting + seed germination\nGrowth promoter\nCytokinin\nCell division + delays senescence + breaks\ndormancy\nGrowth inhibitor\nABA\nStress hormone; stomatal closure +\ndormancy + senescence\nGrowth inhibitor\nEthylene\nGaseous hormone; fruit ripening +\nsenescence\nTRICK - “AGC = Auxin-Gibberellin-Cytokinin = Growth Promoters”\nSource ka direct growth-promoter group.\nTRICK - “AE = ABA-Ethylene = Growth Inhibitors”\nABA stress/dormancy; Ethylene fruit ripening.\nTRICK - “Auxin Apex; Gibberellin Bolting; Cytokinin Cell Division”\nA-G-C function hook.\nEXAM TRAP -\nEthylene = only gaseous plant hormone in source notes; ABA = stress hormone.\n15. Final Exam Traps — 11 Red Flags\n• Reflex = automatic/involuntary; source notes associate it with spinal cord\n• Nervous = electrical; Endocrine = chemical/hormonal\n• Cerebrum memory vs Cerebellum balance\n• Medulla breathing/heart rate vs Hypothalamus appetite/thirst/sleep/temp\n• beta-cells insulin lowers sugar vs alpha-cells glucagon raises sugar\n• Adrenaline = Do or Die hormone\n• Cardiac = striated but involuntary; Masseter strongest; Gluteus maximus largest\n• Skeleton = 206; Femur largest & strongest; Stapes smallest\n• Vertebrae = 7-12-5-1-1; Ribs = 24\n• Skull immovable; Atlas-Axis pivotal; Elbow hinge; Shoulder ball & socket\n• Tropic growth/direction vs Nastic non-growth/direction-independent; Ethylene gaseous, ABA stress\nEXAM TRAP - “Reflex-Electric/Chemical-Brain-Sugar-Muscle-Bones-Joints-Plant”\nChapter ke main confusion zones isi chain mein revise karo.","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.7 Control, Coordination & Movement\nMASTER MEMORY CHAIN — Poora Chapter Ek Saath\nMASTER - 1-Line Revision Chain\n“Nervous C-P-A = CNS brain+cord, PNS 12 cranial+31 spinal, ANS Sympathetic+Parasympathetic; impulse S-R-S-C-M-E; reflex spinal →\nreceptors Ear balance/hear, Eye vision, Skin touch/heat/pain, Nose smell, Tongue taste; Iris pupil, Cornea clear, Lacrimal tears\n\n→\nbrain Fore higher, Mid visual-auditory reflex, Hind automatic; Fore O-C-E-H, Hind P-C-M; Cerebrum memory vs Cerebellum balance,\nMedulla breath-heart, Hypothalamus hunger-thirst-sleep-temp\n endocrine: GH dwarf, thyroid goitre/cretinism, adrenaline Do-or-\n→\nDie, beta insulin DOWN, alpha glucagon UP, testes testosterone, ovaries oestrogen+progesterone\n muscles V-I-I; cardiac\n→\nstriated+involuntary; Masseter strongest, Gluteus largest\n skeleton206; head 8-14-6-1; vertebrae 7-12-5-1-1; Femur\n→\nbiggest/strongest, Stapes smallest, ribs24\n joints Skull none, Atlas-Axis pivot, Elbow hinge, Shoulder ball; synovial lubricates\n\n→\n→\nplant movement Tropic direction/growth vs Nastic no-direction/non-growth; P-G-H-C\n plant hormones AGC promoters = Auxin\n→\napex, Gibberellin bolting, Cytokinin division; AE inhibitors = ABA stress/dormancy + Ethylene gas/fruit ripening.”\nTOP 5: Cerebrum memory vs Cerebellum balance | beta insulin DOWN vs alpha glucagon UP | Cardiac striated but involuntary | 7-\n12-5-1-1 vertebrae | Tropic direction vs Nastic no-direction.","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.8 Reproduction\nREPRODUCTION — MEMORY TRICK SHEET\nGeneral Science · Biology · Chapter 8 · Asexual + Vegetative + Flower + Pollination + Human Reproduction\nYe full notes nahi hai — source chapter ke asexual methods, vegetative examples, flower parts, double fertilisation, pollination\nterms aur male-female reproductive system ke high-confusion facts ko fast recall format mein compress kiya gaya hai.\n1. Asexual vs Sexual — Clone vs Variation\nFeature\nAsexual\nSexual\nParents\nSingle parent (source also notes bisexual)\nTwo parents of different sex\nGametes\nAbsent\nPresent\nFusion\nNo gamete fusion\nFertilisation present\nOffspring\nClone; no variation\nVariation present\nTRICK - “A = Alone + Again Same; S = Sex Cells + Slightly Different”\nAsexual = no gamete fusion + clone.\nSexual = gametes + fertilisation + variation.\nEXAM TRAP -\nClone/no variation = asexual; variation = sexual.\n2. Asexual Routes — F-F-R-B-S-V\n• Fission\n• Fragmentation\n• Regeneration\n• Budding\n• Spore formation\n• Vegetative reproduction\nTRICK - “F-F-R-B-S-V”\nFission-Fragmentation-Regeneration-Budding-Spore-Vegetative.\n3. Fission — Binary vs Multiple\nType\nResult\nExamples\nBinary fission\n2 progenies\nAmoeba, Bacteria, Leishmania, Paramecium\nMultiple fission\nMany progenies\nPlasmodium - malarial parasite\nTRICK - “Binary = 2; Multiple = Many”\nLeishmania binary; Plasmodium multiple.\nEXAM TRAP -\nLeishmania = binary fission; Plasmodium = multiple fission.\n4. Fragmentation - Regeneration - Budding - Spores\nMethod\nExamples\nFragmentation\nSpirogyra, Rhizopus, Ulothrix, Red algae\nRegeneration\nHydra, Planaria\nBudding\nHydra, Yeast\nSpore formation\nChlamydomonas, Rhizopus, Mucor, Puccinia","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.8 Reproduction\nTRICK - “Hydra = R+B”\nHydra dono karta hai: Regeneration + Budding.\nTRICK - “Yeast Buds; Plasmodium Multiplies”\nYeast = budding; Plasmodium = multiple fission.\nEXAM TRAP -\nHydra ko sirf budding ya sirf regeneration mat yaad karo — source mein dono listed hain.\n5. Tissue Culture — M-I-D\n• Tissue culture = Micropropagation = In-vitro propagation\n• Laboratory method\n• Suitable culture medium\n• Used for decorative + disease-free plants in less time\nTRICK - “M-I-D = Micropropagation - In-vitro - Disease-free”\nTeen words ek saath lock karo.\n6. Natural Vegetative Propagation — S-R-L\nPart\nExamples\nStem\nPotato, Sugarcane\nRoot\nRosewood, Sweet potato\nLeaf\nBegonia, Bryophyllum\nTRICK - “Potato Stem - Sweet Potato Root - Bryophyllum Leaf”\nChapter ka most common vegetative trio.\nEXAM TRAP -\nPotato = stem; Sweet potato = root. Dono ko interchange mat karna.\n7. Artificial Vegetative Propagation — Cutting + Layering\nMethod\nExamples\nShoot cutting\nRose, Sugarcane, Banana\nRoot cutting\nLemon, Orange\nLayering\nApple, Pear, Lemon, Strawberry\nTRICK - “Shoot R-S-B; Root L-O; Layer A-P-L-S”\nR-S-B = Rose-Sugarcane-Banana.\nL-O = Lemon-Orange.\nA-P-L-S = Apple-Pear-Lemon-Strawberry.\n8. Flower Parts — C-C-A-G\nPart\nMemory\nCalyx\nGreen part\nCorolla\nColoured part\nAndroecium\nStamens - male organ\nGynoecium\nPistil/Carpel - female organ","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.8 Reproduction\nTRICK - “C-C-A-G = Green - Colour - Male - Female”\nCalyx green, Corolla colour, Androecium male, Gynoecium female.\nTRICK - “A = Andro = Male; G = Gyno = Female”\nMale-female floral organ pair.\n9. Monoecious/Bisexual vs Dioecious — Maize Trap\n• Source bisexual/monoecious examples = Hibiscus, Pea, Rose, Sunflower, Tomato, Mango, Lily\n• Maize = monoecious plant but flowers are unisexual; male + female flowers separate on same plant\n• Dioecious source examples = Papaya, Cucumber, Watermelon, Muskmelon\nTRICK - “Maize = Same Plant, Separate Flowers”\nMonoecious + unisexual flowers.\nTRICK - “P-C-W-M = Separate Plants”\nPapaya-Cucumber-Watermelon-Muskmelon = dioecious in source list.\nEXAM TRAP -\nMaize = monoecious but unisexual flowers; Papaya = dioecious.\n10. Gametogenesis — Micro vs Mega\n• Microsporogenesis = haploid pollen grains in anther\n• Megasporogenesis = haploid embryo sac in ovule\nTRICK - “Micro = Male Pollen; Mega = Female Embryo Sac”\nMicro/anther vs Mega/ovule.\n11. Double Fertilisation — 2n + 3n\nFusion\nProduct\nMale gamete n + Female gamete n\nZygote 2n > Embryo\nMale gamete n + Polar/Secondary nuclei 2n\nEndosperm 3n\nTRICK - “1st = 2n Embryo; 2nd = 3n Endosperm”\nDouble fertilisation source notes mein Angiosperms ki uniqueness hai.\nTRICK - “Ovule Seed; Ovary Fruit”\nAfter fertilisation conversion pair.\nEXAM TRAP -\nEmbryo = 2n; Endosperm = 3n. Ovule = seed; Ovary = fruit.\n12. Pollination Types — A-G-C\nType\nMeaning\nAutogamy\nSame bisexual flower\nGeitonogamy\nDifferent flowers of same plant\nCross-pollination\nDifferent plants","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.8 Reproduction\nTRICK - “Auto = Same Flower; Geito = Same Plant; Cross = Different Plant”\nA-G-C ladder.\nEXAM TRAP -\nSource notes add: Geitonogamy genetically self-pollination-like.\n13. Pollination Agents — A-H-E\nTerm\nAgent\nExamples\nAnemophily\nWind\nMaize, Bamboo, Sugarcane\nHydrophily\nWater\nVallisneria, Hydrilla\nEntomophily\nInsects\nSalvia, Fig, Rose\nTRICK - “A-H-E = Air - H2O - Entomo/Insect”\nAnemo wind, Hydro water, Entomo insects.\nEXAM TRAP -\nAnemophily wind; Hydrophily water; Entomophily insects.\n14. Male Reproductive System — L-S-E-V\nPart / Cell\nFunction\nTestes\nPrimary male organs; sperm + testosterone\nLeydig cells\nTestosterone\nSertoli cells\nNutrition/support of sperms\nEpididymis\nSperm maturation\nVas deferens\nSperm transport\nTRICK - “L = Leydig = Libido/Testosterone; S = Sertoli = Support”\nLeydig testosterone; Sertoli sperm nutrition/support.\nTRICK - “Epididymis = Matures; Vas = Moves”\nMaturation vs transport.\n• Testes located in scrotum, outside abdominal cavity\n• Male accessory glands listed = Prostate, Cowper’s, Perineal\n• Source path components include Seminal vesicle > Urethra > Penis\nEXAM TRAP -\nTestes = scrotum outside abdomen. Leydig/Sertoli interchange mat karna.\n15. Female Reproductive System — O-O-U\nPart\nFunction / Fact\nOvaries\nPrimary female organs; eggs + female hormones\nOviducts\nPair; ovaries to uterus; carry egg\nUterus\nEmbryo development site\n• Ovaries located in abdominal cavity\n• Female hormones listed = Oestrogen + Progesterone\n• Progesterone associated with corpus luteum in source notes\n• Oogenesis = formation of haploid eggs\n• Female accessory glands listed = Bartholin’s + Perineal","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.8 Reproduction\nTRICK - “O-O-U = Ovary - Oviduct - Uterus”\nEgg source > transport > embryo-development site.\n16. Male vs Female — Fast Pairing\nFeature\nMale\nFemale\nPrimary organ\nTestes - scrotum\nOvaries - abdomen\nGamete formation\nSpermatogenesis\nOogenesis\nGamete\nHaploid sperm\nHaploid egg\nHormone side\nLeydig > Testosterone\nCorpus luteum > Oestrogen/Progesterone in\nsource table\nTransport/Maturation\nEpididymis / Vas deferens\nOviduct\nTRICK - “Sperma = Sperm; Oo = Ovum”\nSpermatogenesis male; Oogenesis female; both haploid through meiosis.\nFertilisation > diploid zygote 2n.\n17. Final Exam Traps — 12 Red Flags\n• Leishmania binary fission; Plasmodium multiple fission\n• Hydra = budding + regeneration; Yeast = budding\n• Tissue culture = micropropagation = in-vitro propagation\n• Potato stem; Sweet potato root; Bryophyllum leaf\n• Maize monoecious with unisexual flowers on same plant; Papaya dioecious\n• Double fertilisation: embryo 2n; endosperm 3n\n• Ovule > seed; Ovary > fruit\n• Autogamy same flower; Geitonogamy same plant/different flower; Cross = different plants\n• Anemophily wind; Hydrophily water; Entomophily insects\n• Leydig testosterone; Sertoli sperm nutrition/support\n• Spermatogenesis sperm; Oogenesis egg; both haploid\n• Testes scrotum outside abdomen; ovaries abdominal cavity\nEXAM TRAP - “Fission-Hydra-Culture-Veg-Maize-2n/3n-Seed/Fruit-AGC-AHE-LS-Gametes-Location”\n12 source traps ko isi order mein revise karo.\nMASTER MEMORY CHAIN — Poora Chapter Ek Saath\nMASTER - 1-Line Revision Chain\n“Asexual = alone/no fusion/clone vs Sexual = gametes+fertilisation+variation\n routes F-F-R-B-S-V\n Binary2: Amoeba-Bacteria-\n→\n→\nLeishmania-Paramecium; Multiple many: Plasmodium\n Fragmentation Spirogyra/Rhizopus/Ulothrix/Red; Regeneration Hydra-\n→\nPlanaria; Budding Hydra-Yeast; Spores Chlamydomonas-Rhizopus-Mucor-Puccinia\n Tissue culture = Micropropagation = In-vitro =\n→\ndisease-free\n natural veg: Potato stem, Sweet potato root, Bryophyllum leaf; artificial Shoot R-S-B, Root L-O, Layer A-P-L-S\n Flower\n→\n→\nC-C-A-G = Calyx green, Corolla colour, Andro male, Gyno female; Maize same plant separate unisexual flowers vs Papaya dioecious →\nMicro pollen/anther, Mega embryo-sac/ovule\n Double fertilisation: 2n embryo + 3n endosperm; Ovule seed, Ovary fruit\n\n→\n→\nPollination A-G-C = same flower/same plant/different plants; agents A-H-E = wind/water/insects\n Male: testes scrotum, Leydig\n→\ntestosterone, Sertoli support, Epididymis maturation, Vas transport\n Female: ovaries abdomen, Oestrogen+Progesterone,\n→\nOogenesis, Oviduct transport, Uterus embryo\n Spermatogenesis sperm + Oogenesis egg = haploid; fertilisation = zygote 2n.”\n→\nTOP 5: Leishmania binary vs Plasmodium multiple | Potato stem/Sweet potato root/Bryophyllum leaf | Maize monoecious vs\nPapaya dioecious | Embryo 2n vs Endosperm 3n | Leydig testosterone vs Sertoli support.","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.9 Heredity & Evolution\nHEREDITY & EVOLUTION — MEMORY TRICK SHEET\nGeneral Science · Biology · Chapter 9 · Mendel + Crosses + DNA/RNA + Genetic Diseases + Evolution\nYe full notes nahi hai — source chapter ke Mendelian laws, F2 ratios, inheritance scientists, DNA/RNA facts, genetic disorders,\nevidences aur evolution theories ko fast exam-recall tricks mein compress kiya gaya hai.\n1. Mendel Basics — M-P-7\n• Gregor Johann Mendel = Father of Genetics\n• Experiment plant = Pisum sativum / Garden pea\n• Studied 7 pairs of contrasting characters\n• Heredity = traits ka parent se offspring transmission\n• Genetics = genetic traits aur inheritance ka study\nTRICK - “M-P-7 = Mendel - Pisum - 7 Pairs”\nMendel ke opening facts ka one-line code.\nTRICK - “T-R-Y-P vs D-W-G-W”\nDominant examples: Tall-Round-Yellow-Purple.\nRecessive examples: Dwarf-Wrinkled-Green-White.\n2. Mendel’s 3 Laws — D-S-I\nLaw\nCore Memory\nDominance\nDominant allele recessive ko mask karta hai\nSegregation\nAlleles gametogenesis mein separate\nIndependent Assortment\nOne trait ki inheritance dusre ko affect nahi karti\nTRICK - “D-S-I = Dominance - Segregation - Independent”\nLaw 1, 2, 3 ka direct order.\nTRICK - “Segregation = Purity of Gametes”\nSource ka exact alternate name.\nTRICK - “Mono = Law 1+2; Di = Law 3”\nMonohybrid supports Dominance + Segregation.\nDihybrid gives Independent Assortment.\nEXAM TRAP -\nSegregation aur Independent Assortment ko interchange mat karna.\n3. Monohybrid Cross — 3:1 / 1:2:1\n• P generation = TT Tall x tt Dwarf\n• F1 = all Tt Hybrid Tall\n• F1 self = Tt x Tt\nF2 Ratio\nResult\nPhenotype\n3 : 1 = Tall : Dwarf\nGenotype\n1 : 2 : 1 = TT : Tt : tt\nTRICK - “Mono = P3, G121”\nPhenotype 3:1; Genotype 1:2:1.","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.9 Heredity & Evolution\nEXAM TRAP -\n3:1 phenotype hai; 1:2:1 genotype.\n4. Dihybrid Cross — 9:3:3:1\n• P = RRYY Round Yellow x rryy Wrinkled Green\n• F1 = RrYy Round Yellow\n• F1 gametes = RY, Ry, rY, ry\n• F2 phenotype ratio = 9:3:3:1\nTRICK - “Di = 9-3-3-1”\nRound Yellow : Round Green : Wrinkled Yellow : Wrinkled Green.\nEXAM TRAP - “BbEe x BbEe > BBEE = 1/16”\nSource ka direct probability trap.\n5. Inheritance Scientists — S-B-M\nScientist\nContribution\nSutton & Boveri\nChromosomal theory of inheritance\nT.H. Morgan\nDrosophila experimental verification + gene theory\nT.H. Morgan\nGenes linear on chromosomes like beads\nTRICK - “S-B Theory, Morgan Proof + Line”\nSutton-Boveri = theory.\nMorgan = Drosophila verification + linear gene arrangement.\nEXAM TRAP -\nChromosomal theory = Sutton & Boveri; Morgan = experimental verification/gene theory.\n6. DNA Scientist Timeline — M-HW-WC\nScientist\nYear\nFact\nFriedrich Miescher\n1869\nNucleic acid discovery\nHershey & Chase\n1952\nDNA as genetic material\nWatson & Crick\n1953\nDouble-helix model\nTRICK - “69 Miescher - 52 Hershey/Chase - 53 Watson/Crick”\nDiscovery, proof, model — three different facts.\nEXAM TRAP -\nWatson-Crick = model; Hershey-Chase = genetic material proof.\n7. Nucleoside vs Nucleotide — +P Rule\n• Nucleoside = nitrogenous base + deoxyribose sugar\n• Nucleotide = nucleoside + phosphate\nTRICK - “Nucleotide = Nucleoside + P”\nP = phosphate.","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.9 Heredity & Evolution\n8. DNA Bases — AG Purines / TC Pyrimidines\nGroup\nBases\nPurines\nAdenine (A) + Guanine (G)\nPyrimidines\nThymine (T) + Cytosine (C)\nPairing\nA-T and G-C\nTRICK - “PURe As Gold = A-G”\nPurines = A + G.\nPyrimidines = T + C.\nTRICK - “AT / GC = DNA Couples”\nSource base-pairing rule.\n9. DNA vs RNA — DDT vs SRU\nFeature\nDNA\nRNA\nStrands\nDouble\nUsually Single\nSugar\nDeoxyribose\nRibose\nSpecial base\nThymine\nUracil replaces Thymine\nRNA types\n—\nrRNA ~80%, tRNA 10-15%, mRNA 3-5%\nTRICK - “DNA = Double-Deoxy-Thymine; RNA = Single-Ribose-Uracil”\nD-D-T vs S-R-U.\nTRICK - “rRNA 80 > tRNA 10-15 > mRNA 3-5”\nSource abundance order.\nEXAM TRAP -\nRNA has Uracil, not Thymine.\n10. Gene Expression — R-T-T\n• Central flow in source: DNA > Replication > DNA > Transcription > mRNA > Translation > Protein\nTRICK - “Replicate DNA, Transcribe RNA, Translate Protein”\nR-T-T sequence.\n11. Honeybee Ploidy — Male n, Female 2n\nTRICK - “Male = Mini n; Female = Full 2n”\nHoneybee male haploid; female diploid.\nEXAM TRAP -\nMale honeybee = haploid, female = diploid.\n12. Genetic Diseases — Sex-linked Pair\nDisease\nCause / Type\nKey Fact\nHaemophilia\nX-linked recessive\nA = Factor VIII; B = Factor IX\nColour blindness\nX-linked recessive\nRed-green distinction affected","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.9 Heredity & Evolution\nTRICK - “H-A = 8; H-B = 9”\nHaemophilia-A Factor VIII, Haemophilia-B Factor IX.\nTRICK - “X-linked = Males More Expressed”\nSource reason: males have only one X chromosome.\n13. Sickle Cell — 11 / 6 / Valine\n• Sickle-cell anaemia = beta-globin gene mutation\n• Gene on chromosome 11\n• Valine replaces glutamic acid at 6th position\n• RBC becomes sickle-shaped and carries less O2\nTRICK - “Sickle = 11-6-Val”\nChromosome 11, position 6, Valine.\nEXAM TRAP -\nSickle cell chromosome = 11; substitution at 6th position.\n14. Down - Turner - Klinefelter — 47 / 45 / 47\nSyndrome\nChromosome Pattern\nSex / Cue\nDown\nTrisomy 21; total 47\nAutosomal trisomy\nTurner\n44 + X0; total 45\nFemale\nKlinefelter\n44 + XXY; total 47\nMale\nTRICK - “D-T-K = 47-45-47”\nDown 47, Turner 45, Klinefelter 47.\nTRICK - “Turner = X0 Female; Kline = XXY Male”\nSex-chromosome patterns ko name ke saath lock karo.\nEXAM TRAP -\nDown = Trisomy 21; Turner = 45 female; Klinefelter = 47 male.\n15. Evolution — Descent with Modification\n• Biological evolution = complex organisms ka simple/primitive life forms se development\n• Also called Descent with modification\nTRICK - “Evolution = Descent + Modification”\nSource ka direct alternate phrase.\n16. Fossil Dating — C-U-K\n• Fossils = dead organisms ke remains preserved in primitive rocks of Earth crust\n• Age determination methods = C14 dating, U-Pb dating, K-Ar dating\nTRICK - “C-U-K = Carbon - Uranium/Lead - Potassium/Argon”\nThree dating methods source list se.","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.9 Heredity & Evolution\n17. Homologous vs Analogous vs Vestigial\nType\nStructure/Origin\nFunction\nExample\nHomologous\nSimilar\nDifferent\nForelimbs of vertebrates\nAnalogous\nDifferent\nSimilar\nWings of insects and birds\nVestigial\nFunctional in ancestors\nReduced/non-functional now\nAppendix, wisdom teeth, coccyx\nTRICK - “Homo = Same Structure, Different Job”\nHomologous > divergent evolution.\nTRICK - “Analog = Different Structure, Same Job”\nAnalogous > convergent evolution.\nEXAM TRAP -\nHomologous divergent; Analogous convergent.\n18. Connecting Links — V-E-A\nOrganism\nConnects\nVirus\nLiving - Non-living\nEuglena\nAnimal - Plant\nArchaeopteryx\nReptile - Bird\nTRICK - “V-E-A = Virus Life/Nonlife; Euglena Animal/Plant; Archaeopteryx Reptile/Bird”\nThree direct connecting-link pairs.\n19. Evolution Theories — L-D-V\nScientist\nBook / Experiment\nCore Idea\nLamarck\nPhilosophie Zoologique\nAcquired characters; use & disuse\nDarwin\nOn the Origin of Species, 1859\nNatural selection; struggle; survival of fittest\nHugo de Vries\nEvening Primrose\nMutation theory; sudden inherited changes\nTRICK - “L-D-V = Lamarck Acquired, Darwin Selection, de Vries Mutation”\nTheory-scientist pairing.\nTRICK - “Lamarck = Use/Disuse; Darwin = Natural Selection; Vries = Sudden Mutation”\nCore-idea chain.\nEXAM TRAP -\nDarwin 1859 Origin; Lamarck Philosophie Zoologique; de Vries Evening Primrose.\n20. Final Exam Traps — 15 Red Flags\n• Segregation = Purity of Gametes; Independent Assortment = dihybrid\n• Monohybrid phenotype 3:1; genotype 1:2:1\n• Dihybrid phenotype 9:3:3:1; BBEE probability 1/16 in source cross\n• Sutton-Boveri theory; Morgan Drosophila verification/gene theory\n• Miescher 1869; Hershey-Chase 1952; Watson-Crick 1953\n• RNA = Uracil, not Thymine\n• Honeybee male n; female 2n\n• Haemophilia-A VIII; Haemophilia-B IX","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.9 Heredity & Evolution\n• Sickle cell = chromosome 11, Valine for glutamic acid at 6th\n• Down = Trisomy 21, 47\n• Turner = 44+X0, 45, female\n• Klinefelter = 44+XXY, 47, male\n• Homologous same structure/different function = divergent\n• Analogous different structure/same function = convergent\n• Archaeopteryx reptile-bird; Euglena animal-plant; Virus living-non-living\nEXAM TRAP - “Laws-Ratios-Scientists-RNA-Ploidy-Disease-Evolution”\nChapter ke highest-confusion areas isi chain mein revise karo.\nMASTER MEMORY CHAIN — Poora Chapter Ek Saath\nMASTER - 1-Line Revision Chain\n“M-P-7 = Mendel-Pisum-7 pairs; dominant T-R-Y-P vs recessive D-W-G-W\n laws D-S-I; Segregation=Purity, Mono laws1+2, Di law3\n\n→\n→\nMonohybrid P3/G121; Dihybrid 9-3-3-1; BbEe BBEE=1/16\n Sutton-Boveri theory, Morgan Drosophila proof+linear genes\n DNA\n→\n→\ntimeline Miescher1869 > Hershey-Chase1952 > Watson-Crick1953; nucleoside base+sugar, nucleotide +phosphate; Purines AG,\nPyrimidines TC, pairs AT/GC\n DNA D-D-T vs RNA S-R-U; rRNA80 > tRNA10-15 > mRNA3-5; Replicate-Transcribe-Translate\n\n→\n→\nHoneybee male n/female2n\n Haemophilia A8/B9 + colour blindness X-linked; Sickle 11-6-Val; D-T-K = Down47/21, Turner45-X0-\n→\nfemale, Kline47-XXY-male\n Evolution = descent with modification; fossils C-U-K dating\n Homologous same/different divergent;\n→\n→\nAnalogous different/same convergent; vestigial appendix-wisdom-coccyx\n links V-E-A = Virus life/nonlife, Euglena animal/plant,\n→\nArchaeopteryx reptile/bird\n theories L-D-V = Lamarck acquired/use-disuse, Darwin natural selection/Origin1859, de Vries\n→\nmutation/Evening Primrose.”\nTOP 5: Mono 3:1 & 1:2:1 vs Di 9:3:3:1 | Miescher-Hershey/Chase-Watson/Crick | RNA U not T | D-T-K 47-45-47 | Homologous\ndivergent vs Analogous convergent.","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.10 Human Disease\nHUMAN DISEASE — MEMORY TRICK SHEET\nGeneral Science · Biology · Chapter 10 · Disease Types + Transmission + Pathogens + Vectors + Diagnostic Tests\nYe full notes nahi hai — source chapter ke disease-type distinctions, transmission routes, viral/bacterial/protozoan/fungal/worm\ndiseases, vectors, diagnostic tests aur high-confusion disease-pathogen pairs ko fast exam recall format mein compress kiya gaya\nhai.\n1. Disease Types — C-A-I / Non-Infectious\nType\nSource Meaning / Examples\nCongenital\nGenetic/developmental defects during embryonic development;\ncerebral palsy, haemophilia, colour blindness\nAcquired - Acute\nShort-term; cold, cholera\nAcquired - Chronic\nLong-lasting; TB, elephantiasis, arthritis\nInfectious\nSpread through contact/carrier/medium; pathogens include virus,\nbacteria, protozoa, fungi, worms\nTRICK - “C-A-C-I = Congenital - Acute - Chronic - Infectious”\nAcute = short; Chronic = long.\nInfectious = pathogen + transmission route.\n2. Transmission — 5F Rule\n• Air droplets from coughing/sneezing\n• Contaminated food and water\n• Contaminated blood/body fluids\n• Disease vectors such as flies and mosquitoes\n• Indirect contact through contaminated objects\nTRICK - “5F = Flies - Fingers - Fomites - Food - Fluid”\nSource ka ready-made transmission recall rule.\nEXAM TRAP -\nFomites = contaminated objects ke through indirect contact, source framing ke according.\n3. Non-Infectious Disease Traps — As-Cd-F\nCategory\nExamples / Pair\nAllergies\nHay-fever, Asthma\nNutritional deficiency\nMarasmus-protein; Goitre-iodine; Pellagra-B3; Dental cavities-\nfluoride\nDegenerative\nHeart attack, Arthritis, Diabetes, Glaucoma, Cataract\nMental\nStress, Depression, Epilepsy, Anorexia nervosa\nPollution-related\nArsenic-Black foot; Cadmium-Itai-itai; Fluoride-Knock-Knee\nCancer\nUncontrolled cell growth; Leukemia, Carcinoma, Sarcoma\nTRICK - “As Black - Cd Itai - F Knock”\nArsenic = Black foot disease.\nCadmium = Itai-itai.\nFluoride water = Knock-Knee syndrome.\nEXAM TRAP -\nArsenic/Cadmium/Fluoride disease pairs ko interchange mat karna.","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.10 Human Disease\n4. Viral Diseases — Air, Aedes, Food, Bite, Blood\nDisease\nPathogen\nTransmission\nSmall Pox\nVariola virus\nAir borne\nChicken Pox\nVaricella Zoster\nAir borne\nMeasles / Rubella\nRubellavirus\nAir borne - as stated in source\nInfluenza\nOrthomyxovirus\nAir borne\nSwine Flu\nInfluenza A subtype H1N1\nAir borne\nYellow Fever\nFlavi virus\nAedes aegypti bite\nDengue\nDENV\nAedes aegypti bite\nChikungunya\nCHIKV\nAedes aegypti bite\nPoliomyelitis\nPolio RNA virus type 1,2,3\nContaminated soil/water/food\nRabies / Hydrophobia\nLyssa virus type-1\nInfected animal bite\nAIDS / Slim disease\nRetrovirus HIV\nUnsafe sex, blood/needles/razor, mother-to-\nfoetus\nTRICK - “Aedes = D-C-Y”\nDengue + Chikungunya + Yellow Fever.\nTRICK - “Swine = H1N1; Rabies = Hydrophobia; AIDS = HIV + ELISA”\nThree viral direct-MCQ pairs.\nEXAM TRAP -\nPolio is stated as eradicated in India in the source notes.\n5. Viral Extra Facts — STD + COVID + Jaundice\n• HIV-AIDS, HPV and Hepatitis-B = Sexually Transmitted Diseases in source notes\n• COVID-19 = zoonotic disease caused by SARS-CoV-2; source notes mention animal origin\n• Jaundice = yellow skin; symptom associated with hepatitis/liver inflammation\nTRICK - “STD Trio = HIV - HPV - Hep-B”\nCOVID = SARS-CoV-2.\nJaundice = symptom, source connects it with hepatitis/liver inflammation.\n6. Bacterial Diseases — Air vs Food/Water vs Other\nDisease\nPathogen\nTransmission\nTB\nMycobacterium tuberculosis\nAir borne\nDiphtheria\nCorynebacterium diphtheriae\nAir borne\nWhooping cough\nBordetella pertussis\nAir borne\nPneumonia\nStreptococcus pneumoniae\nAir borne\nCholera\nVibrio cholerae\nContaminated food/water\nTyphoid\nSalmonella typhi\nContaminated food/water; Widal test\nDiarrhoea\nSalmonella sp. + E. coli; source also notes\nRotavirus\nContaminated food/water\nPlague / Black Death\nYersinia pestis\nRat fleas\nTetanus\nClostridium tetani\nWound contamination\nLeprosy / Hansen disease\nMycobacterium leprae\nDirect contact\nTRICK - “Typhoid WIDAL; AIDS ELISA”\nDiagnostic-test pair sabse important.\nTRICK - “TB + Leprosy = Mycobacterium”\ntuberculosis vs leprae species.","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.10 Human Disease\nTRICK - “Plague Rat Flea; Tetanus Lock Jaw; Leprosy Hansen”\nPlague = Black Death.\nTetanus = Lock Jaw / 8th Day disease.\nEXAM TRAP -\nPlague = rat flea, mosquito nahi.\n7. Bacterial Extra — Trachoma + Gastritis\n• Trachoma = eye disease that may lead to blindness; Chlamydia trachomatis\n• Gastritis in stomach = Helicobacter pylori\nTRICK - “Trachoma-Eye-Chlamydia; Gastritis-Stomach-H. pylori”\nLocation + pathogen pair.\n8. Protozoan Diseases — Water + Vector Chain\nDisease\nPathogen\nTransmission\nDiarrhoea\nGiardia intestinalis\nContaminated water/food\nAmoebiasis\nEntamoeba histolytica\nContaminated water/food\nPyorrhoea\nEntamoeba gingivalis\nSaliva / Kiss\nKala-azar\nLeishmania donovani\nSand fly\nMalaria\nPlasmodium sp.\nFemale Anopheles\nAfrican Sleeping Sickness\nTrypanosoma gambiense / T. brucei\nTse-tse fly\nTRICK - “K-M-S = Kala Sand, Malaria Anopheles, Sleeping Tse-tse”\nVector trio: Sand fly > Kala-azar; Female Anopheles > Malaria; Tse-tse > Sleeping sickness.\nEXAM TRAP -\nMalaria = FEMALE Anopheles; not male, not Aedes.\n9. Worm + Fungal Diseases\nGroup / Disease\nPathogen\nTransmission\nAscariasis - Worm\nAscaris lumbricoides\nContaminated fruits/vegetables\nElephantiasis / Filariasis - Worm\nWuchereria bancrofti\nCulex mosquito\nRingworm - Fungal\nMicrosporum + Trichophyton\nDirect/indirect contact\nCandidiasis - Fungal\nCandida\nDirect/indirect contact\nTRICK - “Ascaris = Ascariasis; Wuchereria = Elephantiasis”\nWorm disease pair.\nEXAM TRAP - “Ringworm = FUNGUS, Not Worm”\nMicrosporum + Trichophyton.\n10. Mosquito Master — ADCy / AM / CE\nMosquito\nDisease(s)\nPathogen\nAedes aegypti\nDengue + Chikungunya + Yellow Fever\nDENV, CHIKV, Flavi virus\nFemale Anopheles\nMalaria\nPlasmodium sp.\nCulex\nElephantiasis / Filariasis\nWuchereria bancrofti","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.10 Human Disease\nTRICK - “ADCy = Aedes-Dengue-Chikungunya-Yellow”\nAedes ka three-disease pack.\nTRICK - “AM = Anopheles-Malaria; CE = Culex-Elephantiasis”\nFemale Anopheles = Malaria.\nCulex = Elephantiasis/Filariasis.\nEXAM TRAP -\nAedes, Anopheles aur Culex ke diseases ko mix mat karna.\n11. Other Vectors — S-T-R-D\nVector\nDisease\nPathogen\nSand fly\nKala-azar\nLeishmania donovani\nTse-tse fly\nAfrican Sleeping Sickness\nTrypanosoma gambiense / T. brucei\nRat flea\nPlague / Black Death\nYersinia pestis\nDog/Wolf/Monkey bite\nRabies / Hydrophobia\nLyssa virus type-1\nTRICK - “S-T-R-D = Sand-Kala, Tse-Sleep, Rat-Plague, Dog-Rabies”\nVector-bite chain ko sequence mein yaad karo.\n12. Diagnostic Tests — E-W\nTest\nDisease\nELISA\nAIDS / HIV\nWidal Test\nTyphoid\nTRICK - “E = HIV; W = Typhoid”\nELISA-AIDS/HIV; Widal-Typhoid.\nEXAM TRAP -\nELISA aur Widal ko interchange mat karna.\n13. Pathogen-Wise Master Buckets\nGroup\nDisease Chain from Source\nViral\nSmall/Chicken Pox; Measles/Rubella; Influenza; Swine H1N1;\nDengue; Chikungunya; Yellow Fever; Polio; Rabies; AIDS\nBacterial\nTB; Diphtheria; Whooping cough; Pneumonia; Cholera; Typhoid;\nPlague; Tetanus; Leprosy; Trachoma; Gastritis\nProtozoan\nMalaria; Kala-azar; Amoebiasis; Pyorrhoea; African Sleeping\nSickness\nFungal + Worm\nRingworm; Candidiasis; Ascariasis; Elephantiasis\nTRICK - “V-B-P-FW”\nViral > Bacterial > Protozoan > Fungal/Worm buckets mein disease recall karo.\n14. Final Exam Traps — 12 Red Flags\n• 5F = Flies, Fingers, Fomites, Food, Fluid\n• Arsenic-Black foot; Cadmium-Itai-itai; Fluoride-Knock-Knee\n• Aedes = Dengue + Chikungunya + Yellow Fever\n• Female Anopheles = Malaria","ExamFusion Prep · Biology Memory Tricks · Ch.10 Human Disease\n• Culex = Elephantiasis / Filariasis\n• Sand fly = Kala-azar\n• Tse-tse fly = African Sleeping Sickness\n• Rat flea = Plague / Black Death\n• ELISA = AIDS/HIV; Widal = Typhoid\n• Ringworm = fungal disease, not worm disease\n• Rabies = Hydrophobia; infected animal bite\n• Tetanus = Lock Jaw / 8th Day disease; Leprosy = Hansen disease\nEXAM TRAP - “5F - Pollution - 3 Mosquitoes - 3 Flies/Flea - Tests - Names”\nChapter ke most-asked traps isi order mein revise karo.\nMASTER MEMORY CHAIN — Poora Chapter Ek Saath\nMASTER - 1-Line Revision Chain\n“Disease types C-A-C-I: congenital, acquired acute-short, chronic-long, infectious-pathogen\n transmission 5F = Flies-Fingers-\n→\nFomites-Food-Fluid\n noninfectious pollution: As-Black foot, Cd-Itai-itai, Fluoride-Knock-Knee; cancer uncontrolled growth\n viral:\n→\n→\nSmallpox-Variola, Chickenpox-Varicella, Flu-Orthomyxo, Swine-H1N1, Aedes D-C-Y, Polio contaminated food/water, Rabies\nHydrophobia-Lyssa, AIDS HIV-ELISA; STD HIV-HPV-HepB; COVID SARS-CoV-2; Jaundice hepatitis/liver symptom\n bacterial: TB\n→\nMycobacterium tuberculosis, Typhoid Salmonella-Widal, Cholera Vibrio, Plague Yersinia-rat flea, Tetanus Clostridium-Lock Jaw,\nLeprosy M.leprae-Hansen; Trachoma Chlamydia eye, Gastritis H.pylori\n protozoan: Giardia diarrhoea, Entamoeba\n→\namoebiasis/gingivalis pyorrhoea, Kala-azar Sand-Leishmania, Malaria Female Anopheles-Plasmodium, Sleeping Tse-tse-\nTrypanosoma\n worms: Ascaris ascariasis, Wuchereria filariasis-Culex; fungi: Ringworm Microsporum+Trichophyton, Candidiasis\n→\nCandida\n mosquito code ADCy / AM / CE; other vectors Sand-Kala, Tse-Sleep, Rat-Plague, Dog-Rabies\n tests ELISA-HIV, Widal-\n→\n→\nTyphoid.”\nTOP 5: Aedes D-C-Y | Female Anopheles Malaria | Culex Filariasis | ELISA HIV vs Widal Typhoid | Ringworm = fungus, not worm."];
